#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096841 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096400 :question/id #uuid "19bf8d06-cbcc-547b-b882-ac89cd39fd1a" :question/math-academy-id "e-181" :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = (3x^4+x^3)^5,$ calculate $\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf = g^5, \\qquad g = 3x^4+x^3\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}g} \\cdot \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}g} {\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}g}  = 5g^4,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}g} {\\textrm{d}x} = 12x^3 + 3x^2.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =5{g}^{4}\\cdot (12{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =5(3{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}{)}^{4}(12{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29956ecb-a28a-5406-b8dc-9776e9a3ccee" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097250 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85752216-72d1-52c5-a307-1f7593bb2baa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(x^{4} + 3x)(4x^{3} + 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095425} {:answer/id #uuid "bffd2b2f-d555-5e17-8756-8a754febf526" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(x^{4} + 3x)(4x^{3} + 3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095426} {:answer/id #uuid "eef892e3-6947-592f-9345-311b595e0536" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(4x^{3})(x^{4} + 3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095427} {:answer/id #uuid "05327ffd-b783-558f-8116-edad45453171" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(4x^{3})(x^{4} + 3x)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095428} {:answer/id #uuid "62baa9f7-a4b9-5ff4-84f6-37fdf189f3fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(x^{4} + 3x)(4x^{3} + 3)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095429}] :answer-field/id #uuid "a72fa786-3004-51a5-a233-17a9a216dfb3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095424}] :question/id #uuid "b551e489-4c37-53ea-831b-8f3ca007450b" :question/math-academy-id "q-48625" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{df}{dx}$ when $f(x) = (x^{4} + 3x)^{2}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097325 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c2e9e80-9009-5c4d-8418-53807b048413" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18(4+6x{)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333097} {:answer/id #uuid "2f451149-34e3-5874-ac22-67e5f7f806cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18x(4+6x{)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333429} {:answer/id #uuid "ac1f9842-ca0a-55db-bcb6-06735b728dba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(4+6x{)}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334496} {:answer/id #uuid "b1df2cc8-3e8e-59c7-a92f-f4f573b06622" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(4+6x{)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334551} {:answer/id #uuid "d848947b-033e-583b-9707-793c5c4bf6dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30(4+6x{)}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334883}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c2e9e80-9009-5c4d-8418-53807b048413" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18(4+6x{)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333097} :answer-field/id #uuid "564e943b-61e3-5dd9-b71b-ad37ff586952" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335411}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f0bd56a8-5c99-5166-be8e-4185bfb62ed5" :question/math-academy-id "q-666" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ when $f(x)=(4+6x{)}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf={g}^{3},\\,g=4+6x\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=3{g}^{2},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=6.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =3\\cdot 6\\cdot (4+6x{)}^{2} \\\\  & =18(4+6x{)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235382 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "182d03f6-7007-5d30-9f41-468940bf1b5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8t(2{t}^{2}+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234577}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "182d03f6-7007-5d30-9f41-468940bf1b5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8t(2{t}^{2}+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234577} :answer-field/id #uuid "88d80e90-19de-5aa6-bcad-6a5bb1a4fd17" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235141}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5f760aed-6b31-5953-b3f9-00dcac8b3038" :question/math-academy-id "q-132974" :question/problem "$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}]=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's define a function $h(t):$\n\n$$\nh(t)=(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}\n$$\n\nTo differentiate this function, we use the chain rule. Let \n\n$$\ng(t)=2{t}^{2}+3,\\,h(g(t))=g(t)^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating our functions, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(t) & =(2{t}^{2}+3)^{′}=4t, \\\\ {h}^{′}(g(t)) & =({g}^{2})^{′}=2g.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}] & ={h}^{′}(t) \\\\  & ={h}^{′}(g(t))\\cdot {g}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =2g\\cdot 4t \\\\  & =8t(2{t}^{2}+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250188 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "147b8607-00ab-583f-9d29-c36db3ffb93e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30{(5x+10)}^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247609} {:answer/id #uuid "8b9923c0-68fb-5db2-a270-b22a0a565a94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30{(5x+10)}^{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248673} {:answer/id #uuid "b955e3b8-b464-5b10-97ae-eea2d8b024ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{(5x+10)}^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249080} {:answer/id #uuid "efb057ba-bc50-5a4b-9c8c-026e21fb0356" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10{(5x+10)}^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249528} {:answer/id #uuid "f825b128-d786-54b0-9c93-577427b75d05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{(5x+10)}^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249588}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "147b8607-00ab-583f-9d29-c36db3ffb93e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30{(5x+10)}^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247609} :answer-field/id #uuid "1beb6b75-3505-5fda-ac7d-3d7a94737480" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249718}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "421599a5-1cf3-595e-9d3b-cce9b649fa80" :question/math-academy-id "q-11410" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ when $f(x)={(5x+10)}^{6}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf={g}^{6},\\,g=5x+10\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=6{g}^{5},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=5.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =6{g}^{5}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =30(5x+10{)}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314997 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db185f31-d898-509f-b9c4-a2881a9060fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-28(1-7x)^3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313696}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db185f31-d898-509f-b9c4-a2881a9060fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-28(1-7x)^3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313696} :answer-field/id #uuid "36ad8472-0eaf-547f-a51d-d9fd88b94c6d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314156}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "45dae178-5aad-56cb-99e8-4cae2859e574" :question/math-academy-id "q-340690" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ when $f(x)=(1-7x)^{4}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf={g}^{4},\\,g=1-7x\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=4{g}^{3},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=-7.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =4{g}^{3}\\cdot (-7) \\\\  & =-28(1-7x)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317301 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9dddace1-6902-5bd8-8d9c-4f8e9ee4c695" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316001}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9dddace1-6902-5bd8-8d9c-4f8e9ee4c695" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316001} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb310ac7-3d59-5c34-90b1-fa1d08886eef" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317143}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f5911b4d-cb67-58a7-a1eb-0368289d94d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-342630" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={({x}^{2}+2x+1)}^{2},$ the slope at the point $(0,1)$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule to find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\nFirst, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf={g}^{2},\\,g={x}^{2}+2x+1\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=2g,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=2x+2.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =2g\\cdot (2x+2) \\\\  & =2({x}^{2}+2x+1)(2x+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line at the point where $x=0$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=0} & =[2({x}^{2}+2x+1)(2x+2){]}_{x=0} \\\\  & =2((0{)}^{2}+2\\cdot (0)+1)(2\\cdot (0)+2) \\\\  & =2(1)(2) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317308 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "afdc11cd-82dd-5c6f-bcb3-29722512bae8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-96" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316117}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "afdc11cd-82dd-5c6f-bcb3-29722512bae8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-96" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316117} :answer-field/id #uuid "466922a0-7e46-579f-8acd-8a627c38b832" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316705}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4d9eda9c-9861-5891-97dc-ba4b870f7db4" :question/math-academy-id "q-342663" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={(3x+1)}^{4},$ the slope at the point $(-1,16)$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use chain rule to find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\nFirst, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf={g}^{4},\\,g=3x+1\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=4{g}^{3},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=3.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =4{g}^{3}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =12(3x+1{)}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line at the point where $x=-1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=-1} & =[12(3x+1{)}^{3}{]}_{x=-1} \\\\  & =12(3\\cdot (-1)+1{)}^{3} \\\\  & =12(-2{)}^{3} \\\\  & =-96.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317337 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e15c4f3-e0aa-5296-8996-2def04133195" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6x+11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316002}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e15c4f3-e0aa-5296-8996-2def04133195" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6x+11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316002} :answer-field/id #uuid "adb706a4-b97c-5d9a-a3d9-b9ec8f4ae7e2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316955}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f16d8b27-8720-58fb-ad21-d69be0217bc6" :question/math-academy-id "q-342910" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=(3-2x{)}^{3},$ the equation of the tangent at the point $(2,-1)$ is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule to find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\nFirst, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf={g}^{3},\\,g=3-2x\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=3{g}^{2},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=-2.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =3{g}^{2}\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-6(3-2x{)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line at the point where $x=2$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=2} & =(-6{(3-2x)}^{2}){|}_{x=2} \\\\  & =-6{(3-2(2))}^{2} \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the slope of the tangent line at the point $(2,-1)$ is $m=-6.$ Now, we find the equation of the tangent at that point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y+1 & =-6\\cdot (x-2) \\\\ y & =-6x+12-1 \\\\ y & =-6x+11\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329904 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "354142ae-6999-5699-b81d-9f16570b6115" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-12x+25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327394} {:answer/id #uuid "6637b75c-a39e-5721-a28b-4a320ad5c971" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=12x-23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327821} {:answer/id #uuid "790d805f-3881-5904-ba81-7ea14a53c052" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3x+7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327971} {:answer/id #uuid "85aa1fa8-f1d2-5839-ba72-e02c15010a7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-4x+9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328087} {:answer/id #uuid "bcf17a1f-abef-5a3c-bb11-4d68176c30e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4x-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328579}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6637b75c-a39e-5721-a28b-4a320ad5c971" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=12x-23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327821} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f94fd28-0c54-584b-93ad-2ea2c59a1f97" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329394}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6c44e7b4-d61a-54f6-b052-1c62e5ff035f" :question/math-academy-id "q-48666" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={(5-3x)}^{4},$ find the equation of the tangent at point $(2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule to find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\nFirst, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf={g}^{4},\\,g=5-3x\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=4{g}^{3},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=-3.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =4{g}^{3}\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =-12(5-3x)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line at the point where $x=2$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=2} & =-12{(5-3(2))}^{3} \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the slope of the tangent line at the point $(2,1)$ is $m=12.$ Now, we find the equation of the tangent at that point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =12(x-2) \\\\ y-1 & =12x-24 \\\\ y & =12x-23\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Polynomial Raised to a Power Using the Chain Rule"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096842 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096401 :question/id #uuid "8fcdb2c8-71ca-5bfa-b7fe-49f8f2c05bbb" :question/math-academy-id "e-182" :question/problem "Given that $y = \\sqrt{1-x^2},$ find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}.\n$$\n\nHere, we've used $u$ instead of $g$ for the intermediate variable. \n\nLet $u=1-x^2.$ Then $y = \\sqrt u = u^{1/2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = \\dfrac{1}{2}u^{-1/2} = \\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt u}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}  = -2x.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (-2x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2x}{2\\sqrt{u}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f0cdac9-57b2-5634-8d98-d6adf8a85204" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097251 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "256d8104-e561-50d4-bb47-c84cda5f979d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{(5x-1{)}^{3}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327259} {:answer/id #uuid "92909c43-4d9f-55c2-9367-b2903d4d10f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5x-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328210} {:answer/id #uuid "b84fdb58-69a3-5339-b857-edeff32c5507" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{5x-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328539} {:answer/id #uuid "ccdb8149-860b-5213-8db1-d92c7a922a29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{5x-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328733} {:answer/id #uuid "e32be03e-6bf9-5893-a620-899e6e49abe1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{(5x-1{)}^{3}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328925}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "256d8104-e561-50d4-bb47-c84cda5f979d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{(5x-1{)}^{3}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327259} :answer-field/id #uuid "14eebaf9-3e88-5f2b-82c3-330efcb42e3a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329219}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "854ec833-4bc0-54f0-9210-c708a9342fef" :question/math-academy-id "q-48730" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5x-1}}.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=5x-1.$ Then $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}={u}^{-1/2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=-\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\cdot (5) \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{(5x-1{)}^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097258 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d468059-f4b7-51ec-a214-229418cddeaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{4x - 3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095473} {:answer/id #uuid "ce172954-02de-5948-98e6-063d373c04b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4x - 3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095474} {:answer/id #uuid "dd38fa2b-33de-5df5-aa85-ed357c5eb5e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{4x + 3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095475} {:answer/id #uuid "fd120f87-d6a9-56e7-ae4f-db302afd2580" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4x + 3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095476} {:answer/id #uuid "7f7778bb-8979-5802-bf93-4a409e6e58b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{4x - 3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095477}] :answer-field/id #uuid "84ab9969-a822-5aa8-beea-30e48f66c60f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095472}] :question/id #uuid "ea97309c-a67a-5e7f-aa97-023276ac6669" :question/math-academy-id "q-48846" :question/problem "Given that $y = \\sqrt{4x - 3}$, find $\\frac{dy}{dx}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244040 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27cec937-8f89-509c-89d8-cac2418dad31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12\\sqrt{3-6x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241525} {:answer/id #uuid "48530683-52e3-5b2d-b80c-52c96ea80021" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3-6x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241833} {:answer/id #uuid "6ea096e1-e11d-5b2f-9a38-2f0713ebe775" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{3-6x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242165} {:answer/id #uuid "afef1e77-e7bb-55d4-85fd-246b46e4f2ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3-6x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242713} {:answer/id #uuid "eb52bd30-42b5-564a-b65a-5b27e7b61017" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3-6x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243264}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "afef1e77-e7bb-55d4-85fd-246b46e4f2ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3-6x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242713} :answer-field/id #uuid "beb44a20-d166-5fe6-a7bc-aa72ceec3cb9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243776}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0ce97e32-c8a9-5155-bb70-6cb7385360f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-107291" :question/problem "Given that $y=\\sqrt{3-6x},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=3-6x.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}={u}^{1/2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-1/2}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-6.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (-6) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3-6x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244048 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b523831-f11c-5631-b1d0-55d429e9b570" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241281} {:answer/id #uuid "321ef9b3-dc8b-5b99-b689-dfe8d1c4cf58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241620} {:answer/id #uuid "501d0fb0-7ccb-548e-bb3a-b18b59d46b6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241894} {:answer/id #uuid "8cf3df5e-c66f-522b-838e-722473ef4ed3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242424} {:answer/id #uuid "99c71189-09eb-555b-8519-9b447439afc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242524}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "501d0fb0-7ccb-548e-bb3a-b18b59d46b6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241894} :answer-field/id #uuid "19320ca1-b053-535e-b992-542e4931ff17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243482}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c8b46779-d19f-5cca-987d-5f500a9de334" :question/math-academy-id "q-107360" :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent to the curve $y=\\sqrt{1-4x}$ at the point where $x=-2.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=1-4x.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-4.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{1-4x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=-2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=-2} & ={(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{1-4x}})|}_{x=-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{1-4(-2)}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297398 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb26afef-455b-5297-b622-9e232fa102cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(2x+7)^{3}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296332}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb26afef-455b-5297-b622-9e232fa102cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(2x+7)^{3}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296332} :answer-field/id #uuid "69dfefe3-2eb8-5512-ab7d-eb2788925109" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296855}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2300bd8f-e3d1-544c-8ad1-127cb2f0bedc" :question/math-academy-id "q-314194" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x+7}}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=2x+7.$ Then $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}={u}^{-1/2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=-\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(2x+7{)}^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317312 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c3cdb155-0bd1-58bc-961e-1bfa7707dad6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-3}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-6x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316217}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3cdb155-0bd1-58bc-961e-1bfa7707dad6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-3}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-6x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316217} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd768cbb-f716-59e5-acc7-6f058b68d4ef" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317150}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1f8a23e9-b484-592c-83ed-60ed3cc8b897" :question/math-academy-id "q-342680" :question/problem "Given that $y=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-6x}$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u={x}^{2}-6x.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x-6.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (2x-6) \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x-3)}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-6x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-3}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-6x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317313 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8316ace3-a7b2-57fb-a2e0-d20a2908d622" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{3x+6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315844}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8316ace3-a7b2-57fb-a2e0-d20a2908d622" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{3x+6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315844} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7c696cc-7f33-5839-8d2a-38d27e70f63c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316978}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c93d262c-d38e-53b1-ba9c-9af520b2e82c" :question/math-academy-id "q-342683" :question/problem "Given that $y=\\sqrt{3x+6}$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=3x+6.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{3x+6}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317321 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea0b074e-9124-53da-b940-4a0beab23775" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{(5x+1)^{3}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316419}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea0b074e-9124-53da-b940-4a0beab23775" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{(5x+1)^{3}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316419} :answer-field/id #uuid "cae0bf8f-009f-5cd9-a958-5250470d215a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317025}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "65a56fb3-c803-55e2-851b-d52f4cc805e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-342764" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5x+1}}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=5x+1.$ Then $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}={u}^{-1/2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=-\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{(5x+1)^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317326 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22c57943-65ff-5d5d-81f8-1002a59bb417" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315311}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22c57943-65ff-5d5d-81f8-1002a59bb417" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315311} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e7bce52-e2d7-50a5-a44d-84c8a8cc1d7d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316728}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "febc6c25-71ff-51d9-b851-6f19e65ebcd1" :question/math-academy-id "q-342802" :question/problem "The slope of the tangent to the curve $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3{x}^{2}+12x}}$ at the point where $x=2$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=3{x}^{2}+12x.$ Then $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}={u}^{-1/2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=-\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=6x+12.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\cdot (6x+12) \\\\  & =-\\frac{6x+12}{2\\sqrt{(3{x}^{2}+12x{)}^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=2} & ={(-\\frac{6x+12}{2\\sqrt{(3{x}^{2}+12x{)}^{3}}})|}_{x=2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{6(2)+12}{2\\sqrt{(3(2{)}^{2}+12(2){)}^{3}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{24}{2\\sqrt{{36}^{3}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Radical Function Using the Chain Rule"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096843 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096402 :question/id #uuid "2c37ae73-233d-5e0d-87ff-05cacc6d078b" :question/math-academy-id "e-183" :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1-3x}},$ calculate $f'(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=1-3x$ and $f(g(x)) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{g}} = g^{-1/2}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =({g}^{-1/2}{)}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{2}{g}^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{2{g}^{3/2}}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =(1-3x{)}^{′}=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{g}^{3/2}}\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2{g}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2(1-3x{)}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0a312840-0d35-50bb-94b3-b04778f4efc3" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096972 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd726c27-0178-5cc8-a8b6-82e60d62ac1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(15x^{4} - 8x)(3x^{5} - 4x^{2})^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093807} {:answer/id #uuid "c477f2d6-5ad8-52b3-8fe5-92a70939ea8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(5x^{5} - 8x)(3x^{5} - 4x^{2})^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093808} {:answer/id #uuid "060329dc-443f-5ee6-a297-ff049fb57b80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8(15x^{4} - 8x)(3x^{5} - 4x^{2})^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093809} {:answer/id #uuid "113fc8bd-4460-5e48-a12a-43e5a61ddd92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(15x^{4} - 8x)(3x^{5} - 4x^{2})^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093810} {:answer/id #uuid "5927f4b1-57be-52eb-ac32-2223134b64df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(5x^{4} - 8x)(3x^{5} - 4x^{2})^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093811}] :answer-field/id #uuid "9edef6e7-9845-5317-bcbd-d2c7b7bf754e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093806}] :question/id #uuid "d119ffe6-ff07-57d9-a572-ef180eb72f6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-11414" :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = (3x^{5} - 4x^{2})^{3}$, find $f^{′}(x)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097259 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9bd8424-cd28-568d-ba73-3aa7ea472675" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- ((4x + 3))/(\\sqrt{(2x^{2} + 3x)^{3}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095479} {:answer/id #uuid "38abde75-2551-5055-a50e-d986efdfd2f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4x + 3)/(\\sqrt{(2x^{2} + 3x)^{3}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095480} {:answer/id #uuid "3c031738-7346-5134-8117-9aee7a7c7253" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- ((4x + 3))/(2\\sqrt{(2x^{2} + 3x)^{3}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095481} {:answer/id #uuid "5ee719f1-7581-568a-8881-2ef944019149" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- ((4x + 3))/(\\sqrt[3]{(2x^{2} + 3x)^{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095482} {:answer/id #uuid "7ef48c55-c62b-518e-a659-3edcd2ea1e5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- ((8x + 6))/(\\sqrt{(2x^{2} + 3x)^{3}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095483}] :answer-field/id #uuid "7bd41183-7068-5455-b0e0-8cdceb8831b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095478}] :question/id #uuid "2da730c2-fcbd-535d-85d1-2014785f31eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-48850" :question/problem "Find $f^{′}(x)$ for $f(x) = \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2x^{2} + 3x}}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244041 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b70de32-5fa9-5056-93ca-35a9463faadb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5(3x+4{)}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241712} {:answer/id #uuid "3ec14f92-c87e-5787-a89f-47a342d4236d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(3x+4{)}^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241739} {:answer/id #uuid "5d608479-e85c-5eaa-9d74-3ea2e83804de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20(3x+4{)}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242022} {:answer/id #uuid "8a1ebdf8-e7b7-53d7-92ca-ae1bc7bccfdd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9(3x+4{)}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242395} {:answer/id #uuid "9a91c92b-ab22-552d-a427-31624937e728" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15(3x+4{)}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242530}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a91c92b-ab22-552d-a427-31624937e728" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15(3x+4{)}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242530} :answer-field/id #uuid "2cdfb9ab-5d97-59c1-90d9-2ac765b9ee1d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243521}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5ffe447f-f2a2-5d58-986c-0fb23858e18e" :question/math-academy-id "q-107293" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={(3x+4)}^{5},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=3x+4$ and $f(g(x))=g(x{)}^{5}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =({g}^{5}{)}^{′}=5{g}^{4}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =(3x+4{)}^{′}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =5{g}^{4}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =15(3x+4{)}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317327 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc8b5aee-000a-5a8d-818f-e20c5debd3ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20(4x+3)^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316184}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc8b5aee-000a-5a8d-818f-e20c5debd3ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20(4x+3)^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316184} :answer-field/id #uuid "625a79d7-54d7-570b-aa7c-729d582ac660" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316778}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9ebc59fe-5ccc-59b1-9757-9c760459c2ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-342804" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={(4x+3)}^{5},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=4x+3$ and $f(g(x))=g(x{)}^{5}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =({g}^{5}{)}^{′}=5{g}^{4}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =(4x+3{)}^{′}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =5{g}^{4}\\cdot (4) \\\\  & =20(4x+3{)}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317333 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8817f05b-018c-5853-b6cc-e00880617a94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{x}^{4}-16{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315880}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8817f05b-018c-5853-b6cc-e00880617a94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{x}^{4}-16{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315880} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5ab2d60-fecd-56b2-a6fc-370a03381af0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317093}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2fb4db78-694f-5845-af11-624db7bcff81" :question/math-academy-id "q-342875" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={({x}^{5}-4{x}^{4})}^{2},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=2({x}^{5}-4{x}^{4})($\n{{field-1}}\n$)$" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)={x}^{5}-4{x}^{4}$ and $f(g(x))=g(x)^{2}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =({g}^{2})^{′}=2g, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{5}-4{x}^{4})^{′}=5{x}^{4}-16{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =2g\\cdot (5{x}^{4}-16{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =2({x}^{5}-4{x}^{4})(\\,5{x}^{4}-16{x}^{3}\\,).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317335 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54037730-2600-58cb-b40d-648fd184a076" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{5x-4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315600}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54037730-2600-58cb-b40d-648fd184a076" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{5x-4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315600} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f9abe6a-bb3b-59b2-9f07-73fdde4dcc36" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316583}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6a140a75-4d09-557b-baa7-d7202838dd2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-342883" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\sqrt{5x-4},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=5x-4$ and $f(g(x))=\\sqrt{g(x)}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & ={(\\sqrt{g})}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{g}}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =(5x-4)^{′}=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{g}}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{5x-4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317336 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d665e027-6ab2-5ec3-82b7-84f4dddc219b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-4{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316315}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d665e027-6ab2-5ec3-82b7-84f4dddc219b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-4{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316315} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1297ade-d13c-5373-bff2-2ee35469ee63" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317084}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "49fe03e2-8c48-5c16-b76a-8505b327ec2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-342891" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2{x}^{4}-{x}^{2}+5}},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(2{x}^{4}-{x}^{2}+5{)}^{3}}}($\n{{field-1}}\n$)$" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=2{x}^{4}-{x}^{2}+5$ and $f(g(x))=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{g(x)}}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & ={(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{g}})}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{g}^{3}}}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =(2{x}^{4}-{x}^{2}+5{)}^{′}=8{x}^{3}-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{g}^{3}}}\\cdot (8{x}^{3}-2x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(2{x}^{4}-{x}^{2}+5{)}^{3}}}(\\,x-4{x}^{3}\\,).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339021 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c5aa34c-8035-515e-9a22-0910ed15b1fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32(16x-7{)}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336452} {:answer/id #uuid "8dec9f04-7eaf-5206-a8d3-7559c929a68c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16(8x-7{)}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337312} {:answer/id #uuid "be24eea7-2334-5d10-9f49-6df8807cf6b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16(16x-7{)}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337721} {:answer/id #uuid "d7e1b6c9-7b2d-5b89-b492-99eacd8fa4bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8(8x-7{)}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337907} {:answer/id #uuid "fb4036bb-195b-5a9c-b701-d3dee3bfbc0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32(8x-7{)}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338239}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb4036bb-195b-5a9c-b701-d3dee3bfbc0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32(8x-7{)}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338239} :answer-field/id #uuid "37b56870-5595-52b0-ae23-7f96f4a87f21" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338377}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "217bdf0c-2bc6-585f-ac52-fad4aec9092f" :question/math-academy-id "q-71954" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={(8x-7)}^{4},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=8x-7$ and $f(g(x))=g(x{)}^{4}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =({g}^{4}{)}^{′}=4{g}^{3}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =(8x-7{)}^{′}=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =4{g}^{3}\\cdot (8) \\\\  & =32(8x-7{)}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339023 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d2f6517-e258-513a-b912-2250e7c54e17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15(2+5x{)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336461} {:answer/id #uuid "3b2c8729-231c-5b49-a873-246a32aa4645" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15(2-5x{)}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336588} {:answer/id #uuid "77d335ef-89db-5392-8e96-2e5663d37f72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15(2+5x{)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337111} {:answer/id #uuid "84c34e15-f7f7-51bd-91ad-be1849bcac42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15(2-5x{)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337229} {:answer/id #uuid "ba6755f1-59bc-5601-b289-66317d2b0d63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15(2-5x{)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337682}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba6755f1-59bc-5601-b289-66317d2b0d63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15(2-5x{)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337682} :answer-field/id #uuid "20dca297-5492-547a-957c-959f9e7520f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338339}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ccad926b-6344-5bec-99bc-507d50cda1e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-71956" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={(2-5x)}^{3},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=2-5x$ and $f(g(x))=g(x{)}^{3}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =({g}^{3}{)}^{′}=3{g}^{2}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =(2-5x{)}^{′}=-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =3{g}^{2}\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =-15(2-5x{)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Function Using the Chain Rule and Prime Notation"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100888 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100725 :question/id #uuid "e092e191-84ac-5d9f-b0db-94d628a6154e" :question/math-academy-id "e-525" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\ny' = -\\dfrac{y}{x}, \\qquad x \\neq 0\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f'(x) = -\\dfrac{1}{x^2}$\n\n- Substituting $y = f(x)$ and $y' = f'(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Differentiating $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(\\frac{1}{x})}^{′} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y = \\dfrac{1}{x}$ and $y' = -\\dfrac{1}{x^2}$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x})}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y = \\dfrac{1}{x}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "201edc93-37b4-556a-aaea-4fcf9a01077a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101155 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27a52cd0-ac03-5c8d-a617-3cb7b0126fd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269616} {:answer/id #uuid "5df0e9d5-2b2c-5d79-ab84-ca87885e2473" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270104} {:answer/id #uuid "701a5dbc-2e62-5de3-945b-e34b10bc5f39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270261} {:answer/id #uuid "cccad23a-acfd-50dc-8b43-cda593fadb2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271065} {:answer/id #uuid "d430c687-b7e8-58dd-98eb-e50553cad6a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271134}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5df0e9d5-2b2c-5d79-ab84-ca87885e2473" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270104} :answer-field/id #uuid "64b40622-fbf9-5a3e-8034-8af1ed1a214f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271687}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0b2e480d-7f17-59ba-a02c-a786007bf39b" :question/math-academy-id "q-140631" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{4y}{x},\\,x\\ne 0\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)={x}^{4}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)=5{x}^{5}.$\n- Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement.\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation." :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Differentiating $f(x)={x}^{4},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{4})^{′} \\\\  & =4{x}^{3} \\\\  & \\ne 5{x}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y={x}^{4}$ and ${y}^{′}=4{x}^{3}$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4y}{x} \\\\ (4{x}^{3}) & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4({x}^{4})}{x} \\\\ 4{x}^{3} & =4{x}^{3}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y={x}^{4}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101157 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c14d1ed-5752-5ac0-8ba1-5a643f5dfc35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269665} {:answer/id #uuid "94fc8685-d2f0-5ef2-a7ab-e1b35ff78eb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270574} {:answer/id #uuid "b0466bd8-b819-5e7b-b7cd-9e56708baddc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270841} {:answer/id #uuid "e12cb1db-96c6-55e9-af73-73a5530aeb77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271246} {:answer/id #uuid "e2e78926-ddd2-5989-b665-eac77287af5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271264}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e12cb1db-96c6-55e9-af73-73a5530aeb77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271246} :answer-field/id #uuid "6609800c-a1ac-5b87-bb69-03288bbe55d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271691}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "47b66471-b70f-5509-8368-6a6674b74447" :question/math-academy-id "q-141078" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2y\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)={e}^{2x}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)=2{e}^{2x}$\n- Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Differentiating $f(x)={e}^{2x},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={({e}^{2x})}^{′} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y={e}^{2x}$ and ${y}^{′}=2{e}^{2x}$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}2y \\\\ 2{e}^{2x} & =2{e}^{2x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y={e}^{2x}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272196 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34270485-5bff-52bf-ae41-08b1bee1a1d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269746} {:answer/id #uuid "389a0c65-5adf-5c0f-9d62-a0d6a6c0821b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269783} {:answer/id #uuid "5531b938-deac-5f0d-96bc-e4236e777318" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270026} {:answer/id #uuid "8e825cdc-2aaf-56bb-9c28-3d7dfac2e941" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270505} {:answer/id #uuid "d0121132-7103-5393-80a4-507acd8c4184" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271097}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34270485-5bff-52bf-ae41-08b1bee1a1d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269746} :answer-field/id #uuid "1beb641a-e76c-5ac9-9803-42dc3f2e4a6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271559}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5c28aa3d-73cf-552e-9e05-dfc5bcb67151" :question/math-academy-id "q-141080" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2y\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$\n- Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Differentiating $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\  & \\ne 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Substituting $y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ and ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$ into the original equation, we get a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}2y \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x & \\overset{?}{=}2(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x & \\ne \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the function $y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ is not a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272197 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65720cff-ac67-573c-b355-c946b6d47e2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270173} {:answer/id #uuid "771372c6-f984-51d7-819d-583b075d5f61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270320} {:answer/id #uuid "c019a28a-dcd7-5e5a-a2e2-15357e719fa8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270971} {:answer/id #uuid "c724b081-8081-5843-86e5-29e06d070d0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271023} {:answer/id #uuid "de5a2a25-e529-5bdd-9c7e-749f5a48a47a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271219}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c724b081-8081-5843-86e5-29e06d070d0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271023} :answer-field/id #uuid "48e408fe-eb76-5085-b71a-96030d52904b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271640}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "82065c87-2fb6-51b8-8d32-9c57d0719fb4" :question/math-academy-id "q-141081" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$\n- Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Differentiating $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)}^{′} \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Substituting $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$ and ${y}^{′}=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ into the original equation, we get a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x & \\ne {cos}^{2}⁡2x\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the function $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$ is not a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272198 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fcca0f3-a734-5821-a06d-f8343e70ea2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269534} {:answer/id #uuid "6d5dd004-13d5-581c-aad4-bee0a7e60ec2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270239} {:answer/id #uuid "73a31251-c78f-5484-b652-d7fc86f10971" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270290} {:answer/id #uuid "93bacff5-8f98-5a78-9a0a-9604f419972e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270560} {:answer/id #uuid "fa2bf753-807f-5720-a657-2805a9668a72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271456}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1fcca0f3-a734-5821-a06d-f8343e70ea2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269534} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a79a925-e351-511b-b352-4a48c43fdc1e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271524}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9b7a88f7-a074-5f69-9224-f1585f849cc4" :question/math-academy-id "q-141082" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{e}^{y}}\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$\n- Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Differentiating $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ and ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x}$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{y}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & =\\frac{1}{x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272199 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74ae0ac0-cbd7-5e19-9e62-18c4e30401ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270299} {:answer/id #uuid "7d855e8a-1d57-5875-8d6d-12d0951b8e3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270369} {:answer/id #uuid "7f7151bb-1ac2-5705-85fc-5081794b458e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270380} {:answer/id #uuid "c08033e6-c809-5d66-be80-0f4095dc7570" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270973} {:answer/id #uuid "f54e5a37-096a-5380-9441-b692b0890ac8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271423}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f54e5a37-096a-5380-9441-b692b0890ac8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271423} :answer-field/id #uuid "574997d4-2cf3-5549-972e-17ab6fe6132d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271667}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c4402732-29b4-541b-a6e6-a372fd33bf97" :question/math-academy-id "q-141084" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}},\\,x\\ne 0\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}$\n- Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Differentiating $f(x)=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} \\\\  & \\ne -\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Substituting $y=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ and ${y}^{′}=\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}$ into the original equation, we get a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} & \\ne -\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the function $y=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ is not a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272200 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "027f07cc-cf92-57e1-a7dd-52d7f06d98a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269292} {:answer/id #uuid "28cefc3d-d321-5b03-a62f-d8e2472c48f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269636} {:answer/id #uuid "2fb96c78-c8a0-51e1-a8a5-0e898bc94a78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269696} {:answer/id #uuid "88d6f270-4f72-5d1a-bb5b-8fbb6eb96434" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270449} {:answer/id #uuid "b73d03e8-8fba-5412-a8cc-88b5936c079c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270892}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28cefc3d-d321-5b03-a62f-d8e2472c48f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269636} :answer-field/id #uuid "a051e209-09e1-5e9e-b803-9d96c9cf127a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271797}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "33a5a8c7-40f0-5353-a590-88087e77753a" :question/math-academy-id "q-141086" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-3y\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)=3{e}^{-x}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)=3{e}^{-x}$\n- Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Differentiating $f(x)=3{e}^{-x},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(3{e}^{-x})}^{′} \\\\  & =-3{e}^{-x} \\\\  & \\ne 3{e}^{-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Substituting $y=3{e}^{-x}$ and ${y}^{′}=-3{e}^{-x}$ into the original equation, we get a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}-3y \\\\ -3{e}^{-x} & \\overset{?}{=}-3(3{e}^{-x}) \\\\ -3{e}^{-x} & \\ne -9{e}^{-x}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the function $y=3{e}^{-x}$ is not a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272201 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "496e15c7-53c7-579c-8873-1e0eda230e59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269937} {:answer/id #uuid "5806fb9e-afea-5d8f-9ce2-c668e570e705" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270046} {:answer/id #uuid "ba3be53d-e686-5999-8cae-d77e8bfdbcc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270914} {:answer/id #uuid "dbf24249-8330-5344-9a68-ed06fb6b088d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271194} {:answer/id #uuid "dee82fb9-fbfd-5840-bc3a-4c4b47cb6275" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271223}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "496e15c7-53c7-579c-8873-1e0eda230e59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269937} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1cdc1fa-0584-5f35-a9ea-2a774691d9b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271906}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9c8904e1-5f17-5951-8dbc-61b76cd0babc" :question/math-academy-id "q-141088" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{4x}{{e}^{y/2}}\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)=4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{4x}$\n- Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Differentiating $f(x)=4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{x} \\\\  & \\ne \\frac{1}{4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y=4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ and ${y}^{′}=\\frac{4}{x}$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4x}{{e}^{y/2}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4x}{{e}^{(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)/2}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4x}{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4x}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4x}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{x} & =\\frac{4}{x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y=4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272202 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1da8ca83-9810-5e03-afc4-c10c787af01c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269514} {:answer/id #uuid "9cdd200c-7408-5659-8fe2-7f44e245f0d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270653} {:answer/id #uuid "a2061503-cef9-50bf-a85a-ef2c605b3879" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270708} {:answer/id #uuid "e813d0ad-d2f2-5d00-b765-d4005a112175" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271297} {:answer/id #uuid "eb031019-568e-5c95-9847-afd852126e06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271329}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9cdd200c-7408-5659-8fe2-7f44e245f0d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270653} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2f29670-922a-5956-81fd-e1a1924ed0c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271911}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8806c061-892c-5fd2-baf1-cd903aa5ea31" :question/math-academy-id "q-141089" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{2y}{x},\\,x\\ne 0\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)=-3{x}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)=-5x$\n- Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Differentiating $f(x)=-3{x}^{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(-3{x}^{2})}^{′} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =-6x \\\\  & \\ne -5x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y=-3{x}^{2}$ and ${y}^{′}=-6x$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2y}{x} \\\\ -6x & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2(-3{x}^{2})}{x} \\\\ -6x & =-6x\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y=-3{x}^{2}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Solution to a First-Order Differential Equation"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100889 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100713 :question/id #uuid "cbd0cb3d-cf0f-54db-8cfc-c938e463e4f8" :question/math-academy-id "e-5087" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=2t^2, \\,\\,\\, y=t^2+1, \\quad t \\geq 0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t^2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx=2t^2 \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad  t^2 = \\dfrac{x}{2}\n$$\n\nNow substituting $t^2=\\dfrac{x}{2}$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={t}^{2}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{2}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x+1.$\n\nFinally, since $t \\geq0$ and $x=2t^2,$ we must have $x \\geq 0.$\n\nSo, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{2}x+1, \\quad x \\geq 0 .\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "79107609-77e6-55e9-970d-da63c39daf0a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101207 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28b55e3d-922e-5fd5-a435-05886bb984e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{(\\frac{1+2x}{x})}^{2},\\,x\\ne 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272749} {:answer/id #uuid "8a657071-1650-5d6c-b5c4-92125994bb16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{1-2x}{x})}^{2},\\,x\\ne 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273580} {:answer/id #uuid "94ae029c-717d-594a-94b3-99e945ac9df3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{-1-2x}{x})}^{2},\\,x\\ne 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273683} {:answer/id #uuid "e5c8491d-ffbc-5e21-af51-3dd2104b993d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{1+2x}{x})}^{2},\\,x\\ne 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274417} {:answer/id #uuid "f9e82ab8-5078-5eff-8d80-7c402c1a0adc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{(\\frac{-1+2x}{x})}^{2},\\,x\\ne 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274577}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5c8491d-ffbc-5e21-af51-3dd2104b993d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{1+2x}{x})}^{2},\\,x\\ne 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274417} :answer-field/id #uuid "67afa213-144b-5939-9e4e-014024f2077a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274813}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5acf88e6-b052-5b29-9849-a312529836f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-15678" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{t-2},\\,\\,\\,y={t}^{2},\\,t\\ne 2.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{t-2} \\\\ x(t-2) & =1 \\\\ t-2 & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{x}+2 \\\\ t & =\\frac{1+2x}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow substituting $t=\\frac{1+2x}{x}$ into our expression for $y={t}^{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={(\\frac{1+2x}{x})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y={(\\frac{1+2x}{x})}^{2}.$\n\nFinally, since $t\\ne 2$ and $x=\\frac{1}{t-2},$ we must have $x\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny={(\\frac{1+2x}{x})}^{2},\\,x\\ne 0.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101210 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85aa4d14-80d1-5752-8fd1-0411a67d3ba3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = (x - 2)^{2}, x ∈ (- ∞, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098596} {:answer/id #uuid "88ed67e5-8008-5e9a-bf16-7f21332088a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = x^{2}, x ∈ (- ∞, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098597} {:answer/id #uuid "cb64b154-ef9a-5411-b1ee-b7338acfc194" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = x + 2, x ∈ (- ∞, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098598} {:answer/id #uuid "ec57c426-a179-55be-92bb-f8bfdbc1e791" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = (x + 4)^{2}, x ∈ (- ∞, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098599} {:answer/id #uuid "ed10e3fb-a8f9-5fb6-a491-53e533208b77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = (x + 2)^{2}, x ∈ (- ∞, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098600}] :answer-field/id #uuid "553cbf31-db12-5ac2-aeb1-44860435fe50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098595}] :question/id #uuid "3962e387-0e50-5c09-a43b-458e9ce412cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-15755" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n$x = t - 2, y = t^{2}, t ∈ (- ∞, ∞)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237916 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "166d0a6c-05a8-5786-b48c-f4e93b1f3b0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{2}(x+3)^{2}(x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235955} {:answer/id #uuid "1dc3551c-3d67-5cc6-8438-b49e16bdf115" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{4}(x+3)^{2}(x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236011} {:answer/id #uuid "45c69b36-6d72-503c-9c36-09fae5919dba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{8}(x+3)^{2}(x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236267} {:answer/id #uuid "d00d1ba1-3166-54b8-b47f-c8a1f0daadff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{4}(x+3)(x-3)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237136} {:answer/id #uuid "f55f988f-ed06-5427-b54b-471e782de7ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{8}(x+3)(x-3)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237365}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45c69b36-6d72-503c-9c36-09fae5919dba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{8}(x+3)^{2}(x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236267} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f11615a-9ff4-5a1c-97b7-4c67c3c73fb2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237627}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "440dd157-2982-5c1f-8661-23a8368ce029" :question/math-academy-id "q-102664" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=1-2t,\\,\\,\\,y=(t-2)^{2}(t+1),\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=1-2t\\,⇒\\,t=\\frac{1-x}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=\\frac{1-x}{2}$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={(\\frac{1-x}{2}-2)}^{2}(\\frac{1-x}{2}+1) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1-x-4}{2})}^{2}(\\frac{1-x+2}{2}) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{-x-3}{2})}^{2}(\\frac{3-x}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}(x+3)^{2}(x-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=-\\frac{1}{8}(x+3)^{2}(x-3).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237918 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "77633f7b-a4f6-5605-b197-300dedbccde8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4x(x+5)(2x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236567} {:answer/id #uuid "7b957847-885e-58d3-87c4-376c6913a3f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2(2x+1)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236587} {:answer/id #uuid "878bf002-299b-523a-93bd-80e7642d9000" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4x(2x+1)(2x-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236663} {:answer/id #uuid "8b44f564-28f4-5786-9cf7-4b0382705e60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=(2x+1)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236690} {:answer/id #uuid "abe07822-f677-59f4-b923-e8aa2238417d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2x(x-5)(2x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236904}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77633f7b-a4f6-5605-b197-300dedbccde8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4x(x+5)(2x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236567} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2e38393-de71-5a6b-b9b5-971eae7190b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237668}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "44f36d19-593e-54ad-8307-e8844e507156" :question/math-academy-id "q-102668" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{t-5}{2},\\,\\,\\,y=({t}^{2}-25)(t-4),\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{t-5}{2}\\,⇒\\,t=2x+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=2x+5$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =((2x+5)^{2}-25)(2x+5-4) \\\\  & =(4{x}^{2}+20x+25-25)(2x+1) \\\\  & =(4{x}^{2}+20x)(2x+1) \\\\  & =4x(x+5)(2x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=4x(x+5)(2x+1).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275353 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20505f6c-76c3-5503-9aa4-58967d3492b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=5{x}^{2}+x+2,\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272676} {:answer/id #uuid "80f54d66-49b1-5053-b817-7ebbba893a52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{x}^{2}+10x-24,\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273492} {:answer/id #uuid "be1b076f-fa12-524f-9aeb-e359af08e3c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2{x}^{2}-5x-12,\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274034} {:answer/id #uuid "db025224-4e97-5e03-8743-b73b379a2eea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-4{x}^{2}-2x+5,\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274302} {:answer/id #uuid "e21deb99-1b3a-5d03-bd8b-47900ebde6d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{2}-10x+24,\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274387}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e21deb99-1b3a-5d03-bd8b-47900ebde6d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{2}-10x+24,\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274387} :answer-field/id #uuid "1dcaa63f-4e24-5cd8-972a-532fc5338912" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274690}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "be482fdb-4246-5b2d-93fc-c680fd3bc2e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-15653" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=5-t,\\,\\,\\,y={t}^{2}-1,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=5-t\\,⇒\\,t=5-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=5-x$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(5-x)^{2}-1 \\\\  & =25-10x+{x}^{2}-1 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-10x+24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y={x}^{2}-10x+24.$\n\nFinally, since $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $x=5-t,$ we must have $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSo, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny={x}^{2}-10x+24,\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275354 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20261641-fd84-5f77-86f9-34f0fa33a731" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-x(3+{x}^{2}),\\,x\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272674} {:answer/id #uuid "24a93e7a-6bb2-5462-9150-db02feafe760" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2{x}^{2}(9-{x}^{2}),\\,x\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272708} {:answer/id #uuid "43312442-bb1a-5f99-b317-fe4c73170b3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2x(9-{x}^{2}),\\,x\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272963} {:answer/id #uuid "65eba75f-db0c-5c62-b886-977dc76e3ace" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{2}(9-{x}^{2}),\\,x\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273260} {:answer/id #uuid "8180fde6-b73a-51b5-92cf-81508b0e34d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{2}(3+{x}^{2}),\\,x\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273495}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65eba75f-db0c-5c62-b886-977dc76e3ace" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{2}(9-{x}^{2}),\\,x\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273260} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d6adaab-7830-564f-ac4c-cf11efa896cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274795}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "48edb5ee-f8fa-5b2d-bf8e-0422cc536872" :question/math-academy-id "q-15654" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{t},\\,\\,\\,y=t(9-t),\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\sqrt{t}\\,⇒\\,t={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t={x}^{2}$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={x}^{2}(9-{x}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y={x}^{2}(9-{x}^{2}).$\n\nFinally, since $t\\ge 0$ and $x=\\sqrt{t},$ we must have $x\\ge 0.$\n\nSo, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny={x}^{2}(9-{x}^{2}),\\,x\\ge 0.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275366 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d045052-a873-54a4-84e5-7bf4e6574118" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3x}{1+2x},\\,x\\ne -\\frac{1}{2},\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272644} {:answer/id #uuid "22c4e507-f7c6-5de7-b268-663b56128436" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{1-x},\\,\\,x\\ne 0,\\,1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272694} {:answer/id #uuid "81222995-5d78-5bef-9adf-4e395c20b564" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{1+x},\\,\\,x\\ne -1,\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273494} {:answer/id #uuid "af021638-e540-5191-98d4-73961ba655c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{1-3x},\\,x\\ne 0,\\,\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273899} {:answer/id #uuid "db25591c-9dfe-5ed2-9b3e-5d90e2274d32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3x}{1-2x},\\,x\\ne 0,\\,\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274304}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af021638-e540-5191-98d4-73961ba655c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{1-3x},\\,x\\ne 0,\\,\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273899} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ae43d0f-3f4e-5d8a-ae9f-f9aea258ba0a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274789}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d29a5f16-5e4f-594a-98bd-3d918fb3b468" :question/math-academy-id "q-15677" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{t+1},\\,\\,\\,y=\\frac{1}{t-2},\\,t\\ne -1,\\,2.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{1}{t+1}\\,⇒\\,t=\\frac{1}{x}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow substituting $t=\\frac{1}{x}-1$ into our expression for $y$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{((\\frac{1}{x}-1)-2)} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{x}-3)} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1-3x}{x})} \\\\ y & =\\frac{x}{1-3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\frac{x}{1-3x}.$\n\nFinally, since $t\\ne -1,\\,2$ and $x=\\frac{1}{t+1},$ we must have $x\\ne 0,\\,\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nSo, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{x}{1-3x},\\,x\\ne 0,\\,\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292916 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f98dd494-7f2f-57dc-af90-42867eb57511" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-2x+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291685}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f98dd494-7f2f-57dc-af90-42867eb57511" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-2x+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291685} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5c7a55c-b5a8-5ece-9653-1e4addb15710" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292338}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "adc18662-35c2-5630-b6c2-775bfa727c41" :question/math-academy-id "q-276277" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=t+1,\\,\\,\\,y={t}^{2}+4,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=t+1\\,⇒\\,t=x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=x-1$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(x-1)^{2}+4 \\\\ y & ={x}^{2}-2x+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is \n$y={x}^{2}-2x+5.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292918 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "efebc768-607d-542f-b7b0-0c55ce311093" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291635}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "efebc768-607d-542f-b7b0-0c55ce311093" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291635} :answer-field/id #uuid "49ec9008-738f-5560-b1c0-bab64077c3f0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291970} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27cbd4cc-c040-570c-8981-361a2c723b70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4x+1}{9x+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290796}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27cbd4cc-c040-570c-8981-361a2c723b70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4x+1}{9x+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290796} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd2e0a7d-9fb9-581d-bf54-ae428568db6f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292355}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3e3af460-d835-5ff0-b7d7-10fb0256d9da" :question/math-academy-id "q-276284" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{t-4},\\,\\,\\,y=\\frac{t}{2t+1},\\,t\\ne 4,\\,\\,\\,t\\ne -\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,x\\ne $\n{{field-2}}$,\\,\\,\\,x\\ne 0.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{t-4}\\,⇒\\,t=\\frac{4x+1}{x}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=\\frac{4x+1}{x}$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{(\\frac{4x+1}{x})}{(2\\cdot \\frac{4x+1}{x}+1)} \\\\ y & =\\frac{(\\frac{4x+1}{x})}{(\\frac{8x+2}{x}+1)} \\\\ y & =\\frac{(\\frac{4x+1}{x})}{(\\frac{9x+2}{x})} \\\\ y & =\\frac{4x+1}{\\cancel{x}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{x}}{9x+2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{4x+1}{9x+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\frac{4x+1}{9x+2}.$\n\nFinally, since $t\\ne -\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=\\frac{1}{t-4}$ has range $x\\ne 0,$ we must have \n$x\\ne -\\frac{2}{9},\\text{ }0.$ \n\nTherefore, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{4x+1}{9x+2},\\,x\\ne -\\frac{2}{9},\\,\\,\\,x\\ne 0.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299913 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a1c86cb-411d-534e-810a-79bce25d9b55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297663}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a1c86cb-411d-534e-810a-79bce25d9b55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297663} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c103235-e1c7-50cf-97f5-b61725f0ce9e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298789}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0c26af88-186d-5e0b-b417-96d3412f3929" :question/math-academy-id "q-320871" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=t-2,\\,\\,\\,y={t}^{2}-4,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=t-2\\,⇒\\,t=x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=x+2$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(x+2)^{2}-4 \\\\ y & ={x}^{2}+4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is \n$y={x}^{2}+4x.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299919 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd8f4c87-04b9-5012-91a0-2fa1e73539ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1-3x}{7x-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298449}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd8f4c87-04b9-5012-91a0-2fa1e73539ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1-3x}{7x-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298449} :answer-field/id #uuid "d10035ab-a533-5291-9723-0bc6c8afa230" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299345} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5584afb0-f812-5610-86a2-9366973652b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297913}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5584afb0-f812-5610-86a2-9366973652b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297913} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2071233-48c1-53b8-9e87-ce0c7ce6bb94" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299350}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "80110151-a320-56a4-96e5-9888e94599a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-320926" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{t+3},\\,\\,\\,y=\\frac{t}{-2t+1},\\,t\\ne -3,\\,\\,\\,t\\ne \\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,x\\ne $\n{{field-2}}$,\\,\\,\\,x\\ne 0.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{t+3}\\,⇒\\,t=-3+\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=-3+\\frac{1}{x}$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{(-3+\\frac{1}{x})}{(-2\\,(-3+\\frac{1}{x})+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-3+\\frac{1}{x})}{(6-\\frac{2}{x}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-3+\\frac{1}{x})}{(7-\\frac{2}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3x+1}{\\cancel{x}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{x}}{7x-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-3x}{7x-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\frac{1-3x}{7x-2}.$\n\nFinally, since $t\\ne \\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=\\frac{1}{t+3}$ has range $x\\ne 0,$ we must have \n$x\\ne \\frac{2}{7},\\text{ }0.$ \n\nTherefore, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1-3x}{7x-2},\\,x\\ne \\frac{2}{7},\\,\\,\\,x\\ne 0.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299923 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52d81c8c-e7c3-5d18-9722-ffa9b4fbde0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(3x+1)(x-1)(2-3x)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297902}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52d81c8c-e7c3-5d18-9722-ffa9b4fbde0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(3x+1)(x-1)(2-3x)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297902} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3f57b3d-a654-5285-ac31-3e8cdc988821" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299186}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "49a7b1ee-edb5-5cfe-b0e7-c77c9612e0b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-321074" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{t+1}{3},\\,\\,\\,y=({t}^{2}-4)(1-t)^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{t+1}{3}\\,⇒\\,t=3x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=3x-1$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =((3x-1)^{2}-4){(1-(3x-1))}^{2} \\\\  & =(9{x}^{2}-6x+1-4)(2-3x)^{2} \\\\  & =(9{x}^{2}-6x-3)(2-3x)^{2} \\\\  & =3(3{x}^{2}-2x-1)(2-3x)^{2} \\\\  & =3(3x+1)(x-1)(2-3x)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=3(3x+1)(x-1)(2-3x)^{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299924 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e6c8835-3261-554a-afbb-957028b9b565" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32x(2x-1)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297684}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e6c8835-3261-554a-afbb-957028b9b565" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32x(2x-1)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297684} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4679020-39ab-52d5-99d1-edaaf51fac68" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299460}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4084c354-7618-56fc-b268-f7034497a330" :question/math-academy-id "q-321075" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1-t}{4},\\,\\,\\,y=({t}^{2}-1)(1+t)^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{1-t}{4}\\,⇒\\,t=1-4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=1-4x$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =((1-4x)^{2}-1)((1+(1-4x))^{2}) \\\\  & =(1-8x+16{x}^{2}-1)((2-4x)^{2}) \\\\  & =(-8x+16{x}^{2})(4)(1-2x)^{2} \\\\  & =8x(2x-1)(2-4x)^{2} \\\\  & =8x(2x-1)\\cdot 4(1-2x)^{2} \\\\  & =32x(2x-1)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=32x(2x-1)^{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299925 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f5593662-8d5b-547d-8bc2-6520dd120c28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{2}-1)(5-{x}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298596}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f5593662-8d5b-547d-8bc2-6520dd120c28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{2}-1)(5-{x}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298596} :answer-field/id #uuid "baa6ee25-0549-5c84-9148-4b6a5918be3e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299270}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "78ac25c2-7c10-5369-8ba8-e6189caf0a34" :question/math-academy-id "q-321084" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{\\,t+1\\,},\\,\\,\\,y=t(4-t),\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,x\\ge 1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\sqrt{\\,t+1\\,}\\,⇒\\,t={x}^{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t={x}^{2}-1$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =({x}^{2}-1)(4-({x}^{2}-1)) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-1)(5-{x}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=({x}^{2}-1)(5-{x}^{2}).$\n\nFinally, since $t\\ge 0$ and $t={x}^{2}-1,$ we must have ${x}^{2}-1\\ge 0,$ i.e., $x\\ge 1.$\n\nTherefore, the complete Cartesian equation is $y=({x}^{2}-1)(5-{x}^{2}),\\,x\\ge 1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299926 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d744dcd4-0a3e-511e-b60c-7b2673712e59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{2}+27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298478}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d744dcd4-0a3e-511e-b60c-7b2673712e59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{2}+27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298478} :answer-field/id #uuid "8387e755-e4a8-5276-9d88-37aa96b73bfd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299081}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7fac9ad7-3b23-560c-8033-d32b15e29ec7" :question/math-academy-id "q-321090" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-9},\\,\\,\\,y=3{t}^{2},\\,t\\ge 3.\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,x\\ge 0.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for ${t}^{2}:$\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-9}\\,⇒\\,{t}^{2}={x}^{2}+9\n$$\n\nNow, substituting ${t}^{2}={x}^{2}+9$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=3({x}^{2}+9)=3{x}^{2}+27.\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=3{x}^{2}+27.$\n\nFinally, since $t\\ge 3$ and $x=\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-9},$ we must have $x\\ge 0.$\n\nTherefore, the complete Cartesian equation is $y=3{x}^{2}+27,\\,x\\ge 0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299929 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d45792f3-0a28-56c8-a67d-3d50eaf93eb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298464}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d45792f3-0a28-56c8-a67d-3d50eaf93eb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298464} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b761f73-9699-5009-890b-c604bb787aa6" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298735} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fba2eb2c-542e-5741-9da4-b68fe7825059" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(1+x)^{2}}{4{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298625}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fba2eb2c-542e-5741-9da4-b68fe7825059" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(1+x)^{2}}{4{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298625} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb33573b-f770-5250-8223-d49dca5de7e1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299433}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ce51648f-7c56-5fd1-a391-8983dd27aa88" :question/math-academy-id "q-321141" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2t+1},\\,\\,\\,y=(t+1)^{2},\\,t\\ne -\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,x\\ne $\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2t+1}\\,⇒\\,2t+1=\\frac{1}{x}\\,⇒\\,t=\\frac{1}{2x}-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=\\frac{1}{2x}-\\frac{1}{2}$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={(\\frac{1}{2x}-\\frac{1}{2}+1)}^{2} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2x}+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1+x}{2x})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1+x)^{2}}{4{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\frac{(1+x)^{2}}{4{x}^{2}}.$\n\nFinally, since $t\\ne -\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=\\frac{1}{2t+1}$ we must have $x\\ne 0.$\n\nTherefore, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{(1+x)^{2}}{4{x}^{2}},\\,x\\ne 0.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302062 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3625268-1369-5f41-a534-a0649eceb53b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300856}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3625268-1369-5f41-a534-a0649eceb53b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300856} :answer-field/id #uuid "20d409ef-248e-5eab-a01b-2a42296f4315" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301330} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2d9df89-b63c-55a8-bd14-62b985a73272" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{x-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301091}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2d9df89-b63c-55a8-bd14-62b985a73272" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{x-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301091} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4a0ae5c-3a22-5796-9f64-eda5a7edb186" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301951}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6b94a700-bd85-58aa-bc0c-7585b2834748" :question/math-academy-id "q-321410" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=-3t+2,\\,\\,\\,y=-\\frac{1}{t},\\,t\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,x\\ne $\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=-3t+2\\,⇒\\,t=\\frac{2-x}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=\\frac{2-x}{3}$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{1}{(\\frac{2-x}{3})} \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{x-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\frac{3}{x-2}.$\n\nFinally, since $t\\ne 0$ and $x=-3t+2$ , we must have $x\\ne 2.$ \n\nTherefore, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{3}{x-2},\\,x\\ne 2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335960 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "085c2edd-3369-5dc5-a8be-7a04e4833c75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{x-5},\\,x\\ne 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333070} {:answer/id #uuid "23ef77a2-3fdc-5a4c-8d16-fa9808fc0f18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{2}{x},\\,x\\ne 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333321} {:answer/id #uuid "392acd54-3425-5339-9d3d-6a019cf5800e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} 