/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}(x-9)(x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377042}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33526b37-cab0-5457-9e07-446f0c05aa08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}(x-9)(x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377042} :answer-field/id #uuid "e36531d7-495c-5197-b612-e0e22cd3b3b5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377041}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "22eb775d-0ea8-57a9-a014-0d8618741de6" :question/math-academy-id "q-276278" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=2t+5,\\,\\,\\,y=(t-2)(t+1),\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=2t+5\\,⇒\\,t=\\frac{x-5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=\\frac{x-5}{2}$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(\\frac{x-5}{2}-2)(\\frac{x-5}{2}+1) \\\\  & =(\\frac{x-5-4}{2})(\\frac{x-5+2}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{x-9}{2})(\\frac{x-3}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(x-9)(x-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377085 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e76ec15d-4235-56b9-b033-fb60f2aa5631" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}(x+16)(x-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377087}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e76ec15d-4235-56b9-b033-fb60f2aa5631" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}(x+16)(x-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377087} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4e1d208-e4d2-5915-83c1-b4b15806c88a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377086}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "081bb8fa-7465-5289-83b2-398e18ea3051" :question/math-academy-id "q-320878" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=3t-1,\\,\\,\\,y=(t+5)(t-2),\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=3t-1\\,⇒\\,t=\\frac{x+1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=\\frac{x+1}{3}$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(\\frac{x+1}{3}+5)(\\frac{x+1}{3}-2) \\\\  & =(\\frac{x+1+15}{3})(\\frac{x+1-6}{3}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{x+16}{3})(\\frac{x-5}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}(x+16)(x-5).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Curve Defined Parametrically"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100890 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100714 :question/id #uuid "f3d396ea-caed-51ed-a4d8-0bfc3301f2f3" :question/math-academy-id "e-5101" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=\\csc{t},\\,\\,\\, y=2\\cot{t}, \\quad 0 < t < \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{csc}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡t=x \\\\ y=2\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cot}⁡t=\\frac{y}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the Pythagorean identity $\\sin^{2}{t}+\\cos^{2}{t}=1$ by $\\sin^{2}{t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t}+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t} & =\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\ 1+{(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t})}^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t})}^{2} \\\\ 1+{cot}^{2}⁡t & ={csc}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\csc{t} = x$ and $\\cot t =\\dfrac{y}{2}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+{(\\frac{y}{2})}^{2} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & ={x}^{2}-1 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4({x}^{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y^2 = 4\\left( x^2-1 \\right).$\n\nFinally, since $0 < t < \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ and $x=\\csc t,$ the interval on the $x$-axis is $x \\geq 1.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "78dbecb4-68d4-53b0-b47f-f37ce92da896" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101208 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11e05f86-8f81-5b3b-8cbf-09bc7298eb8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{x+1}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y-4}{5})}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272549} {:answer/id #uuid "41070dd5-9a71-5c9d-9d49-0625efd1a709" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{x-1}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y+4}{5})}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272941} {:answer/id #uuid "9c93177b-838f-57ab-b798-85239c0d52fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{x+4}{5})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y-1}{2})}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273743} {:answer/id #uuid "a3cb9f8c-23b7-50ba-90d6-f90b45e32f80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{x+4}{5})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y+1}{2})}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273799} {:answer/id #uuid "cbde9ca8-dffe-5a3f-aa7f-4f8da9c67b9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{x-1}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y-4}{5})}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274161}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11e05f86-8f81-5b3b-8cbf-09bc7298eb8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{x+1}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y-4}{5})}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272549} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c253336-332a-5d61-90d0-7dcad87c9060" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274655}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "20bf63df-5014-5d31-baeb-e6e95f136242" :question/math-academy-id "q-15751" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1,\\,\\,\\,y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{x+1}{2} \\\\ y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{y-4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{x+1}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y-4}{5})}^{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is ${(\\frac{x+1}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y-4}{5})}^{2}=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101209 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d7810ca-e33f-5d3f-8f99-cc35e16c36f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{y}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{x}{3})}^{2}=2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272648} {:answer/id #uuid "8e37f2c5-9169-53fe-b71d-d0dfd5d3531e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{(\\frac{y}{3})}^{2}+{(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273615} {:answer/id #uuid "c4037945-4011-53b4-b0b4-00e87ba49959" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274088} {:answer/id #uuid "e44ee7b9-47c6-5cb0-a376-c176b5ec298d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{y}{3})}^{2}+{(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274406} {:answer/id #uuid "ebf8aa37-6b95-5381-b808-fe4e44945127" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{y}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{x}{3})}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274471}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e44ee7b9-47c6-5cb0-a376-c176b5ec298d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{y}{3})}^{2}+{(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274406} :answer-field/id #uuid "070cfa41-26cd-55dc-a980-842b8db620c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274648}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0582dce7-ef1a-5cfc-9ee5-447a325b4a34" :question/math-academy-id "q-15752" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{y}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{y}{3})}^{2}+{(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is ${(\\frac{y}{3})}^{2}+{(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237924 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00c514a4-b735-5009-834b-1d072893e4d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+5)^{2}+(y+5)^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235802} {:answer/id #uuid "ac43d246-0ad4-50d7-a08c-dcc18bfc8622" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-5)^{2}+{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236910} {:answer/id #uuid "b257feea-cffc-57ee-b376-7b4c60f365de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+(y-5)^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236946} {:answer/id #uuid "b681d134-42e1-5feb-bb0c-5e1b842860e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+(y+5)^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236973} {:answer/id #uuid "ebbed621-4c37-57a9-8a63-6518b7ef1e20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+5{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237301}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b681d134-42e1-5feb-bb0c-5e1b842860e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+(y+5)^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236973} :answer-field/id #uuid "725f1b0d-a9d2-512e-a325-17a097de05d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237589}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3951ff1d-9cb6-525c-8fd5-1e1ec4a7dbb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-102680" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-5,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=x \\\\ y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-5\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=y+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1$, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+(y+5)^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is ${x}^{2}+(y+5)^{2}=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237925 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2663afa7-56a2-5ebd-ae11-a924299d9710" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{x-3}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y+5}{3})}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236061} {:answer/id #uuid "6395d88b-eecc-5b2d-9be3-8baa352e1b03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{x+2}{5})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y-3}{3})}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236449} {:answer/id #uuid "80ccf35d-bc6a-5313-875a-745c0fe7950f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{x-3}{5})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y+2}{3})}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236619} {:answer/id #uuid "f1c3ae7b-b354-5d77-a6b4-9ab7a0b0e1ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{x-3}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y+2}{5})}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237341} {:answer/id #uuid "f45c9dd1-e74e-58da-9234-d134303089a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{x-5}{3})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y+3}{2})}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237359}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80ccf35d-bc6a-5313-875a-745c0fe7950f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{x-3}{5})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y+2}{3})}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236619} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4533903-3cfe-53ab-953a-991cc8be771f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237690}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a6c004f0-b68c-5924-ab03-701737917e3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-102682" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+3,\\,\\,\\,y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+3\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{x-3}{5} \\\\ y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{y+2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{x-3}{5})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y+2}{3})}^{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is ${(\\frac{x-3}{5})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y+2}{3})}^{2}=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237926 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00b92d5a-d1df-55d9-b76c-8340b0cdd31c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235801} {:answer/id #uuid "17fbd976-22a0-5f3c-a91e-40fd34a09bba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235964} {:answer/id #uuid "8e805a00-40af-55fc-a317-73b147f7007a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1-{x}^{2}}{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236720} {:answer/id #uuid "9fc2f2ab-bdce-542e-b352-c5b04b7f8a95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236815} {:answer/id #uuid "d6fd5bc7-78fe-503f-ab40-8f0d8119f8a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1-{x}^{2}}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237178}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17fbd976-22a0-5f3c-a91e-40fd34a09bba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235964} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e7f2652-5876-52bb-86dd-90e0f34762b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237607}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fd551375-bd59-5d04-876f-c42f8931f73d" :question/math-academy-id "q-102687" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,0<t<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=x \\\\ y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡t=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡t & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t & =\\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe used here that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t>0$ in the range $0<t<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nNow, using the definition of a tangent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡t=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}=\\frac{\\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡t}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=x$ and $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t=y$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}{x}.\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}{x}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275355 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43a576b4-dbc2-516f-9901-f746379954a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+3)^{2}+{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272964} {:answer/id #uuid "7e316ea2-1019-55c9-a067-cecdfe63f010" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+(y-3)^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273467} {:answer/id #uuid "9e4cf287-d278-504a-b64e-8eeec0d74fac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273751} {:answer/id #uuid "b89ab806-011e-5406-a808-5b128fde42c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273987} {:answer/id #uuid "fb9cde1a-2675-5ec3-a089-6d7b9de7a6ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-3)^{2}+(y-3)^{2}=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274593}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "43a576b4-dbc2-516f-9901-f746379954a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+3)^{2}+{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272964} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd871a7c-ba5a-5bd1-afa1-25df185701c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274993}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f8fb649b-135a-5767-aa99-6984e334015e" :question/math-academy-id "q-15657" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-3,\\,\\,\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-3\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=x+3 \\\\ y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1$, we get\n\n$$\n(x+3)^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $(x+3)^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275367 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31312107-5d20-5059-be6e-aeb601b77635" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-3)^{2}+{x}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272827} {:answer/id #uuid "57a8e77f-308a-55ad-9d7f-77cf10ab871b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-2)^{2}+(x-3)^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273139} {:answer/id #uuid "a651a81a-956c-5cd0-8505-74bb046a9688" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-3)^{2}+(x+2)^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273828} {:answer/id #uuid "bdba81d4-1945-5f2a-9580-c45dbea81055" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+3)^{2}+(x+2)^{2}=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274030} {:answer/id #uuid "c0d63d29-beab-5500-ba21-73963177aaa9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}+(x+3)^{2}=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274059}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a651a81a-956c-5cd0-8505-74bb046a9688" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-3)^{2}+(x+2)^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273828} :answer-field/id #uuid "eaeb2b7a-6391-5362-aa64-8478d2f2bdb9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275048}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e0840b2f-51f6-58d5-a7a1-bacf7531f834" :question/math-academy-id "q-15679" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2,\\,\\,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+3,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=x+2 \\\\ y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+3\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=y-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n(y-3)^{2}+(x+2)^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $(y-3)^{2}+(x+2)^{2}=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275401 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0469c29d-f1ef-59a5-bfcc-782cc2ba60f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{4x}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272424} {:answer/id #uuid "40ca55ef-caec-5749-8663-28bf0fb87145" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272938} {:answer/id #uuid "482fbc33-6330-5256-b98a-c29f3b150952" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2x}{\\sqrt{2+{x}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273002} {:answer/id #uuid "a5890c75-2ab4-532e-8278-1e895d973fdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273820} {:answer/id #uuid "fc42ea52-aaff-5a03-877a-cb2a40083453" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274603}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a5890c75-2ab4-532e-8278-1e895d973fdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273820} :answer-field/id #uuid "8749f9b8-216b-5df1-b8f7-c2d19f3cb2f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274866}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "051f821d-0a77-5a88-85da-69a3ef815c14" :question/math-academy-id "q-15759" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,0<t<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=x \\\\ y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡t=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡t & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t & =\\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe used here that $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t>0$ in the range $0<t<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nNow, using the definition of a tangent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡t=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡t}}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=x$ and $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t=y$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275402 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0acb48e3-a0f4-5f8b-bbb7-6c9d4783161d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=1+{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272487} {:answer/id #uuid "4cdf6eeb-a167-56f0-9f69-2fe01b7a765d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=1-{(\\frac{x}{3})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273034} {:answer/id #uuid "527417b5-ca09-55bd-8cef-9f36a0172b21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=1-3{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273087} {:answer/id #uuid "7245d2d7-07cf-5143-a442-4a776b066122" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=1+{(\\frac{x}{3})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273369} {:answer/id #uuid "dae312dd-cd12-5ee0-b6ac-2fb17d244ef5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=1+{(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274300}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7245d2d7-07cf-5143-a442-4a776b066122" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=1+{(\\frac{x}{3})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273369} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b4cd8be-3670-5350-989d-e79c6c272026" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274907}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3486395c-c23b-50d0-ac86-019f33fdad15" :question/math-academy-id "q-15761" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cot}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡t,\\,t\\in (0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=3\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cot}⁡t=\\frac{x}{3} \\\\ y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡t=y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1$ by ${sin}^{2}⁡t$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t}+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t} & =\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\ 1+{(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t})}^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t})}^{2} \\\\ 1+{cot}^{2}⁡t & ={csc}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cot}⁡t=\\frac{x}{3}$ and $\\operatorname{csc}⁡t=y$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+{(\\frac{x}{3})}^{2}={y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is ${y}^{2}=1+{(\\frac{x}{3})}^{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290547 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a02222e-7bac-556f-8a59-fb96cc7379fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288430} {:answer/id #uuid "54e72178-1f7b-5214-9ba6-6c902e98a7d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3(1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288618} {:answer/id #uuid "86ae557a-8091-5236-b106-cb6c715bfdf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3(1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288940} {:answer/id #uuid "870e697f-06aa-529c-aeec-3d2b85541b6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2(1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288942} {:answer/id #uuid "bd1d45d8-de11-5e5a-b944-94183a2a9ac0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289279}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54e72178-1f7b-5214-9ba6-6c902e98a7d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3(1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288618} :answer-field/id #uuid "80f53a4c-1f61-543b-a7a8-834f7fc0d410" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290012}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "92fe000e-8b10-5f6c-a7a8-e9bb8ce2fa57" :question/math-academy-id "q-24378" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using double angle formula for cosine and the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =(1-{sin}^{2}⁡t)-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{x}{2}$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{3}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{3} & =1-2{(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{y}{3} & =1-2(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}) \\\\ \\frac{y}{3} & =1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2} \\\\ y & =3(1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=3(1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290552 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ea4864c-b306-57e1-b44c-21b26487fe84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288575} {:answer/id #uuid "99103521-803c-5804-a165-119989cc93da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289057} {:answer/id #uuid "c1f66a3c-9003-56a0-98dc-04353efeac62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289315} {:answer/id #uuid "c824007d-3cd4-55bb-995f-0ebfb53b60b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3{x}^{2}+8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289358} {:answer/id #uuid "e69d7548-01ac-54b0-b01f-7d230602a3d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289563}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ea4864c-b306-57e1-b44c-21b26487fe84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288575} :answer-field/id #uuid "0da5f05f-ddec-577d-bb96-889893b10755" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289760}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "50632a56-05a3-5b2c-a470-4387daec2008" :question/math-academy-id "q-24574" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{x}{4} \\\\ y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using double angle formula for cosine and the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-(1-{cos}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{x}{4}$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{2}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{2} & =2{(\\frac{x}{4})}^{2}-1 \\\\ \\frac{y}{2} & =2(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16})-1 \\\\ \\frac{y}{2} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-1 \\\\ y & =2(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=2(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-1).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290553 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19c635f8-a836-5e71-b883-8a1bd5cf25e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288196} {:answer/id #uuid "2bacbcf5-685c-51b1-88e0-85a788eecc31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288318} {:answer/id #uuid "87cc2f4e-738c-52b7-abe2-f860d6aed289" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288948} {:answer/id #uuid "9086facf-67d8-55b3-ba11-e7e20d058362" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=7(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288999} {:answer/id #uuid "d34b06b8-fbc5-51ef-94d2-add58e6f047b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{9}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289432}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d34b06b8-fbc5-51ef-94d2-add58e6f047b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{9}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289432} :answer-field/id #uuid "468c38a2-26d6-518c-b2b0-6ef0ade60949" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289886}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ca1f80dd-221f-5c68-b74a-e39a400a3763" :question/math-academy-id "q-24575" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=-\\frac{x}{3} \\\\ y=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using double angle formula for cosine and the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-(1-{cos}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=-\\frac{x}{3}$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{4}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{4} & =2{(-\\frac{x}{3})}^{2}-1 \\\\ \\frac{y}{4} & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{9}-1 \\\\ y & =4(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{9}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=4(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{9}-1).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290554 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54369419-cb7a-53a4-ad10-1cef5f94f9d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-5+\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288612} {:answer/id #uuid "7e3315c5-e581-5704-8feb-0fa7e40d938b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1+\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288885} {:answer/id #uuid "af7ea6ab-b4ce-5db0-93b2-7941e17fe43e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=5+{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289193} {:answer/id #uuid "e910a38f-f4e4-5977-9d9f-7ed4f36b6aae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-18+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289579} {:answer/id #uuid "f6b76b41-917c-54ab-ab82-87ce9743027a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{5}-{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289660}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54369419-cb7a-53a4-ad10-1cef5f94f9d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-5+\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288612} :answer-field/id #uuid "85d607bd-1288-57e3-afbf-e5c537389a08" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290024}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fd03cb1f-af7d-59db-bf84-fa1a13cb0a30" :question/math-academy-id "q-24576" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{x}{6} \\\\ y=-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=-\\frac{y}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using double angle formula for cosine and the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =(1-{sin}^{2}⁡t)-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{x}{6}$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=-\\frac{y}{5}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{y}{5} & =1-2{(\\frac{x}{6})}^{2} \\\\ -\\frac{y}{5} & =1-2(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}) \\\\ -\\frac{y}{5} & =1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{18} \\\\ y & =-5+\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=-5+\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{18}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299922 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "816fa6c2-f991-5039-a41c-4fa48bc8c757" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}{x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298109}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "816fa6c2-f991-5039-a41c-4fa48bc8c757" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}{x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298109} :answer-field/id #uuid "059e198d-f61d-5e53-b612-06a829c8b2fd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298656}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4923000b-5c5c-55b7-9b95-5a7082500328" :question/math-academy-id "q-320946" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n{{field-1}}$=1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{x}{3} \\\\ y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=y+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{x}{3})}^{2}+(y+2)^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}{x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302048 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c03cb41-8dae-5697-aca1-1503571603b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{12{x}^{2}}{25}+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299993}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c03cb41-8dae-5697-aca1-1503571603b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{12{x}^{2}}{25}+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299993} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc50beaf-4f1b-5232-ade9-c9d9974970f5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301930}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "746bed7b-e090-56a7-9ad1-4c0bfac39908" :question/math-academy-id "q-321314" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{x}{5} \\\\ y=-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=-\\frac{y}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the double-angle formula for cosine and the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-(1-{cos}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{x}{5}$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=-\\frac{y}{6}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{y}{6} & =2{(\\frac{x}{5})}^{2}-1 \\\\ -\\frac{y}{6} & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{25}-1 \\\\ y & =-6(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{25}-1) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{12{x}^{2}}{25}+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=-\\frac{12{x}^{2}}{25}+6.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302049 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16bbeffa-db1c-5b1d-ad63-3a7384e31729" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{18}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300050}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16bbeffa-db1c-5b1d-ad63-3a7384e31729" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{18}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300050} :answer-field/id #uuid "34ad1466-19e3-5aaa-b651-784e21d815fe" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301394}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3eac432e-a9f4-5e40-ae91-1f28e72debdf" :question/math-academy-id "q-321318" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{x}{6} \\\\ y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the double-angle formula for cosine and the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-(1-{cos}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{x}{6}$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{5}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{5} & =2{(\\frac{x}{6})}^{2}-1 \\\\ \\frac{y}{5} & =2(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36})-1 \\\\ \\frac{y}{5} & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{36}-1 \\\\ y & =5(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{18}-1) \\\\ y & =\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{18}-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{18}-5.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375624 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b587af18-df81-5db4-88ae-e75eb49804be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}={(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375626} {:answer/id #uuid "42dbc938-1217-5910-be74-c7acaaa09ee3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}={(\\frac{2}{x})}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375627} {:answer/id #uuid "d880a9fe-7ede-58b5-95dc-503280226675" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}={(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375628} {:answer/id #uuid "c09ce9e9-e44a-5f6f-a0ba-e4f831714695" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}={x}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375629} {:answer/id #uuid "394ebbf2-c2d3-501a-9d24-4f85814641c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=1-{(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375630}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d880a9fe-7ede-58b5-95dc-503280226675" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}={(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375628} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5fabfe3-808f-535a-8196-05aa4185848e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375625}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8b4d7dbc-be4c-5d12-8115-af652723cf38" :question/math-academy-id "q-102677" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,t\\in (0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡t=\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡t=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1$ by ${cos}^{2}⁡t$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡t}{{cos}^{2}⁡t}+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡t}{{cos}^{2}⁡t} & =\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\ {(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t})}^{2}+1 & ={(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t})}^{2} \\\\ {tan}^{2}⁡t+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t=\\frac{x}{2}$ and $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t=y$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}+1={y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is ${y}^{2}={(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}+1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377082 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "444c47c5-f6ff-5993-9810-f2a59d46e7f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}+(y-4)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377084}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "444c47c5-f6ff-5993-9810-f2a59d46e7f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}+(y-4)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377084} :answer-field/id #uuid "505822d3-763e-5bdd-b961-64b75dcd3eb1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377083}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1c9558ce-37ee-554b-ae06-97c014ed01c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-319828" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+4,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n{{field-1}}$=1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+4\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=y-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-4)^{2}+{(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}+(y-4)^{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377088 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dfd39e46-6e10-5277-9820-57dd084b55b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{8}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377090}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dfd39e46-6e10-5277-9820-57dd084b55b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{8}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377090} :answer-field/id #uuid "3733f8ba-81a2-59af-8065-3da7adf791ea" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377089}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e0deecc2-15f7-5c26-991c-e45282132b63" :question/math-academy-id "q-321315" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=-\\frac{x}{4} \\\\ y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the double-angle formula for cosine and the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-(1-{cos}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=-\\frac{x}{4}$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{3}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{3} & =2{(-\\frac{x}{4})}^{2}-1 \\\\ \\frac{y}{3} & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{16}-1 \\\\ y & =3(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-1) \\\\ y & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{8}-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{8}-3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377091 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c99283b5-3b7a-5fc6-a4f3-9fb25f6b4ff5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377093}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c99283b5-3b7a-5fc6-a4f3-9fb25f6b4ff5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377093} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7e25b52-ed40-5d5d-a435-71863801ad29" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377092}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6f647799-4d25-58ea-a46d-69aef277766e" :question/math-academy-id "q-321400" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cot}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=2\\operatorname{csc}⁡t,\\,0<t<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=3\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cot}⁡t=\\frac{x}{3} \\\\ y=2\\operatorname{csc}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡t=\\frac{y}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${csc}^{2}⁡t=1+{cot}^{2}⁡t$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{csc}^{2}⁡t & =1+{cot}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe used here that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡t>0$ and $\\operatorname{cot}⁡t>0$ in the range $0<t<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nNow, solving for $y$, we obtain\n\n$$\ny=2\\sqrt{\\,1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}\\,}.\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is \n$y=2\\sqrt{\\,1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}\\,}.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Curve Defined Parametrically With Trigonometric Functions"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100893 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100728 :question/id #uuid "dbb4e0a0-55ec-5cc7-9617-99cec8108940" :question/math-academy-id "e-540" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $y' = 5y$ that satisfies $y(0)=3.$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution, and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =5y \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =5 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =5\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =5\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & =5x+C\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the exponential function to both sides, we find the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\pm {e}^{5x+C} \\\\  & =\\pm {e}^{C}{e}^{5x} \\\\  & =K{e}^{5x}\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K=\\pm e^{C}$ is an arbitrary constant. \n\nNow we apply the condition $y(0) = 3$ to find $K\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =3 \\\\ K{e}^{5\\cdot 0} & =3 \\\\ K & =3\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the particular solution of $y' = 5y$ that satisfies the condition $y(0)=3$ is\n\n$$\ny = 3 e^{5x}  \\,.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "99112850-043d-53e5-8d61-fecb0317bd11" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101401 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "446c4857-dd0c-5efd-afb0-2ea4ed0660a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{3}=\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324526} {:answer/id #uuid "748ec2ec-3297-5615-86ab-bc1f21ac26f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{3}={x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324942} {:answer/id #uuid "9c45be7d-d1cc-59a4-8002-8c72c910bd2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{3}=3{x}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325297} {:answer/id #uuid "d5becbca-f53a-5866-bb37-73887a660a89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{3}=\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325829} {:answer/id #uuid "e7e878f5-c14b-5673-a208-7eea879de351" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{3}={x}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326013}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c45be7d-d1cc-59a4-8002-8c72c910bd2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{3}=3{x}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325297} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6850fb7-3a0b-5d41-af17-c643ce765bf0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326610}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6c894685-37ff-5d18-b9c8-6edb0d037176" :question/math-academy-id "q-4019" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2x}{{y}^{2}}$ that satisfies $y(2)=\\sqrt[3]{13}.$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find the particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2x}{{y}^{2}} \\\\ {y}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x \\\\ \\int {y}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{3}}{3} & ={x}^{2}+{C}_{1} \\\\ {y}^{3} & =3{x}^{2}+3{C}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the general solution is\n\n$$\n{y}^{3}=3{x}^{2}+C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C=3{C}_{1}$  is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(2)=\\sqrt[3]{13}$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\sqrt[3]{13})}^{3} & =3(2)^{2}+C \\\\ 13 & =12+C \\\\ C & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the particular solution of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2x}{{y}^{2}}$ that satisfies the condition $y(2)=\\sqrt[3]{13}$ is\n\n$$\n{y}^{3}=3{x}^{2}+1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101545 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "531c9876-41a6-5528-8716-512d9860a650" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349207} {:answer/id #uuid "63de5349-a542-5177-bb56-e3b3bd658803" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349360} {:answer/id #uuid "86d94f8e-237b-5f46-bbef-54ff8f75c5cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349675} {:answer/id #uuid "89e3ffe6-f402-5afd-82f1-aa385f26d411" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349698} {:answer/id #uuid "d835efde-95ab-5abd-b768-7494d10b73eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350340}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63de5349-a542-5177-bb56-e3b3bd658803" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349360} :answer-field/id #uuid "375659ba-c2ed-5aba-9b69-1aebcdb68de1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350802}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "525ed303-6429-5295-90a0-f24212406b6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-8310" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}$ that satisfies $y(1)=2.$ Assume that $x>0.$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find the particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -\\frac{1}{y} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{y} & =-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAssuming $x>0,$ the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+C},\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(1)=2$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & =2 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+C} & =2 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{C} & =2 \\\\ C & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the particular solution of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}$ that satisfies the condition $y(1)=2$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{-1}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-\\frac{1}{2})}=\\frac{2}{1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}\\,.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235520 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b45fa11b-7aeb-5dbf-a888-002c910fdf5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25+3{e}^{-0.03t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234823}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b45fa11b-7aeb-5dbf-a888-002c910fdf5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25+3{e}^{-0.03t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234823} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba2c9880-594d-5761-a31f-7e56aaee7f89" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235200}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cdb03268-a0ae-53d3-84de-7af60bc110d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-168078" :question/problem "Let $\\overset{^}{T}(t)$ be a particular solution of the differential equation from the previous part that satisfies the condition $\\overset{^}{T}(0)=28.$ Find $\\overset{^}{T}(t).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\overset{^}{T}(t)\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Previously, we found that the general solution is\n\n$$\nT=25+K{e}^{-0.03t}.\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now apply the condition $T(0)=28$ to find $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}28 & =25+K\\,{e}^{-0.03(0)} \\\\ 28 & =25+K\\,{e}^{0} \\\\ K & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the required particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{T}(t)=25+3{e}^{-0.03t}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240789 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2402a50b-0ada-5a95-bd1c-d60ffe3a7054" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y=-2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238371} {:answer/id #uuid "3113425a-30f8-5c8b-88b3-34e913ccf225" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡y=-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238481} {:answer/id #uuid "79eaa63a-7157-5816-ab3b-72d58acb2790" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡y=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239106} {:answer/id #uuid "f7406111-8e32-5b08-aa41-0f61204a9031" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡y=x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240235} {:answer/id #uuid "fd71d029-d6be-50c5-9b70-7d320b3399c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡y=-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240297}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd71d029-d6be-50c5-9b70-7d320b3399c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡y=-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240297} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1159eaa-cd25-57b9-b6f3-0999eae2bb5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240700}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1086559f-da78-5256-be5a-8d4d9777777a" :question/math-academy-id "q-104055" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}$ that satisfies $y(0)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find the particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡y & =x+C \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡y & =-x-C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(0)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-0-C \\\\ 0 & =0-C \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we get the particular solution in implicit form:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡y=-x\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275103 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01b271c8-930d-55d2-b6c9-4d10415d1f40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272398} {:answer/id #uuid "453026c0-09cc-5849-a51c-b0765a6a347c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272980} {:answer/id #uuid "7f4bfc4f-7d7a-5012-b1f2-ad8906f9fa0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273481} {:answer/id #uuid "a9c5c442-efee-51ea-b4c4-619423884639" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273854} {:answer/id #uuid "dcd30bde-3736-5aa2-a5e8-1c3be5c7c83c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274328}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01b271c8-930d-55d2-b6c9-4d10415d1f40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272398} :answer-field/id #uuid "6bfbd7fa-8ea2-5c3c-b15b-9c490e6cfc9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274817}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c2219a64-4fca-5417-a4ef-4988c9db8d90" :question/math-academy-id "q-14310" :question/problem "Find the specific solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}+1$ given that  $y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find the particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}+1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}+1}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}+1}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{arctan}⁡(y) & =x+C \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x+C).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x+C).\n$$\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1$ to find $C$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+C) \\\\ \\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1) & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{\\pi }{4} & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+C \\\\ C & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ C & =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the particular solution of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}+1$ that satisfies the condition $y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275104 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "161701b8-cab3-5a52-baee-ca387468d340" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-1+x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272583} {:answer/id #uuid "50b36c20-41be-54bd-8252-8418beace6e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-{e}^{-x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273071} {:answer/id #uuid "6737182b-f42e-57d4-b8d6-67a37fb50f8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{{e}^{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273273} {:answer/id #uuid "7771e672-13a1-5981-b9ae-117088631933" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-1-{e}^{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273404} {:answer/id #uuid "db69fd4d-8719-5651-b5ad-029bab24e359" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-1+{e}^{-x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274311}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "50b36c20-41be-54bd-8252-8418beace6e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-{e}^{-x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273071} :answer-field/id #uuid "62ccf029-8cdb-5f2e-b996-01a4b881873e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274806}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dd2fa0cf-e14d-5280-8a92-ce27abb0aa11" :question/math-academy-id "q-14312" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y{e}^{-x}$ that satisfies $y(0)={e}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find the particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y{e}^{-x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{-x} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,dx & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & =-{e}^{-x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the exponential function to both sides, we find the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|} & ={e}^{-{e}^{-x}+C} \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{-{e}^{-x}+C} \\\\ y & =\\pm {e}^{-{e}^{-x}+C} \\\\ y & =K{e}^{-{e}^{-x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is an arbitrary constant. \n\nWe now apply the condition $y(0)={e}^{-1}$ to find $K:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-1} & =K{e}^{-{e}^{-0}} \\\\ {e}^{-1} & =K{e}^{-1} \\\\ K & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the particular solution of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y{e}^{-x}$ that satisfies the condition $y(0)={e}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-{e}^{-x}}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275105 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "184695d3-9029-5e71-b2c0-44b31db02e5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{3}=3{x}^{2}+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272598} {:answer/id #uuid "1bb941ed-69f9-51b5-84ab-9b185febcd77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{3}={x}^{2}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272625} {:answer/id #uuid "664df1fa-7210-55f4-b6c5-5c6c644b8caa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{3}={x}^{3}+7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273263} {:answer/id #uuid "7bb836b0-ad07-50fc-aaa1-78966b0fe6f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{3}=5{x}^{3}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273448} {:answer/id #uuid "93347f2d-2098-56c3-8973-3c2341f180fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{3}=2{x}^{3}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273667}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "664df1fa-7210-55f4-b6c5-5c6c644b8caa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{3}={x}^{3}+7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273263} :answer-field/id #uuid "46fc4299-9139-57f9-a4a0-55185f85b3c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274754}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c2372277-7745-54d3-839c-0b3553acea76" :question/math-academy-id "q-14313" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}$  that satisfies $y(1)=2.$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find the particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}} \\\\ {y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ \\int {y}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{3}}{3} & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+{C}_{1} \\\\ {y}^{3} & ={x}^{3}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere   $C=3{C}_{1}$ is an arbitrary constant.  \n\nWe now apply the condition $y(1)=2$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{3} & ={1}^{3}+C \\\\ 8-1 & =C \\\\ C & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we get the particular solution in implicit form:\n\n$$\n{y}^{3}={x}^{3}+7.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319204 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dadf5567-9e34-5988-9b93-ee86c9c672c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{4x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318296}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dadf5567-9e34-5988-9b93-ee86c9c672c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{4x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318296} :answer-field/id #uuid "52b5a75f-9489-53f8-a2ef-2d765ab6e701" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318602}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0dfecf65-ff44-5a31-9ad0-4e0918756475" :question/math-academy-id "q-347853" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2{y}^{2}=0$\nthat satisfies the condition $y(0)=2.$\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find the particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2{y}^{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y & =2\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -\\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int 2\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{y} & =2x+C \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2x+C}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)=\\frac{1}{2x+C},\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(0)=2$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =\\frac{1}{2(0)+C} \\\\ C & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)=\\frac{2}{4x+1}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323684 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8afe1cc-6a73-59a7-a3aa-86abc400ccd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{cos}(4x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322470}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8afe1cc-6a73-59a7-a3aa-86abc400ccd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{cos}(4x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322470} :answer-field/id #uuid "549d1fa9-dbbb-5a28-9ec0-95d21eef029c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323329}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6a3b691d-ce23-5509-8fc2-d490e3df8940" :question/math-academy-id "q-355525" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3y){y}^{′}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)=0$ that satisfies $y(\\frac{\\pi }{8})=0.$ Assume that $-\\frac{\\pi }{6}<y<\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\n$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3y)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3y)\\,\\text{d}y & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3y)\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3y) & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+{C}_{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3y) & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=3{C}_{1}.$\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(\\frac{\\pi }{8})=0$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\frac{\\pi }{8}) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot 0) & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8})+C \\\\ 0 & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+C \\\\ 0 & =0+C \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the particular solution in implicit form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3y) & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323685 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a897ef12-2c09-5096-a8b5-ee77680d8d9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{2}+2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322468}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a897ef12-2c09-5096-a8b5-ee77680d8d9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{2}+2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322468} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea786b33-77be-5d8b-adac-7883d2cd2257" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323631}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e4bca2e9-3423-5b05-8658-606cf78e9ed4" :question/math-academy-id "q-355528" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4x\\sqrt{y}$\nthat satisfies the condition $y(1)=9.$  Assume that $y>0.$\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find the particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =4x\\sqrt{y} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\\,\\text{d}y & =4x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int 4x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 2\\sqrt{y} & =2{x}^{2}+{C}_{1} \\\\ \\sqrt{y} & ={x}^{2}+\\frac{{C}_{1}}{2} \\\\ y & ={({x}^{2}+\\frac{{C}_{1}}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAssuming $y>0,$ the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)=({x}^{2}+C)^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $C=\\frac{{C}_{1}}{2}$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(1)=9$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9 & =((1)^{2}+C)^{2} \\\\ 9 & =(1+C)^{2} \\\\ 3 & =1+C \\\\ C & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)=({x}^{2}+2)^{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323686 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96ae4175-e85f-5fa0-9c66-e85a778d8d55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{7}\\operatorname{tan}(7x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322350}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96ae4175-e85f-5fa0-9c66-e85a778d8d55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{7}\\operatorname{tan}(7x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322350} :answer-field/id #uuid "aef3a27e-eb31-5e68-9379-c97a63bec369" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323508}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "64377a61-5efe-5da5-9c93-affa5fd1266f" :question/math-academy-id "q-355544" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation ${sec}^{2}⁡(4y){y}^{′}={sec}^{2}⁡(7x)$ that satisfies $y(0)=0.$ Assume that $-\\frac{\\pi }{8}<y<\\frac{\\pi }{8}.$\n\n$\\operatorname{tan}⁡(4y)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sec}^{2}⁡(4y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={sec}^{2}⁡(7x) \\\\ {sec}^{2}⁡(4y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={sec}^{2}⁡(7x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {sec}^{2}⁡(4y)\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int {sec}^{2}⁡(7x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(4y) & =\\frac{1}{7}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(7x)+{C}_{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡(4y) & =\\frac{4}{7}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(7x)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=4{C}_{1}.$\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(0)=0$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡(4\\cdot 0) & =\\frac{4}{7}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(7\\cdot 0)+C \\\\ 0 & =0+C \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the particular solution in implicit form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(4y) & =\\frac{4}{7}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(7x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323690 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2e265e1-c3cb-5fab-b4f5-ccf995516d4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{{x}^{2}/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322433}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2e265e1-c3cb-5fab-b4f5-ccf995516d4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{{x}^{2}/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322433} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b3e560d-f350-5b7b-bd75-6283cbcfd045" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323221}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bfb5c5d2-d9d1-5d15-b3d0-8a3479b9922a" :question/math-academy-id "q-355562" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=xy$  that satisfies $y(0)=1.$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find the particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =xy \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\,x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+C\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the exponential function to both sides, we find the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|} & ={e}^{{x}^{2}/2+C} \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{{x}^{2}/2+C}\\, \\\\ y & =\\pm {e}^{{x}^{2}/2+C} \\\\ y & =K{e}^{{x}^{2}/2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is an arbitrary constant. \n\nWe now apply the condition $y(0)=1$ to find $K:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =K{e}^{0} \\\\ K & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the particular solution of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=xy$ that satisfies the condition $y(0)=1$ is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{{x}^{2}/2}\\,.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323694 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec80c9e4-ade4-588f-871a-59a453ae5b9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2-1/x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323008}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec80c9e4-ade4-588f-871a-59a453ae5b9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2-1/x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323008} :answer-field/id #uuid "afe21afb-2492-52bc-ac68-6f00ab45aa76" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323510}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "debef920-e2c6-5181-8631-87e6b6d975ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-355578" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}=0$  that satisfies $y(1)=e.$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find the particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\,\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & =-\\frac{1}{x}+C\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the exponential function to both sides, we find the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|} & ={e}^{-1/x+C} \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{-1/x+C}\\, \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{-1/x}\\cdot {e}^{C} \\\\ y & =K{e}^{-1/x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is an arbitrary constant. \n\nWe now apply the condition $y(1)=e$ to find $K:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e & =K{e}^{-1} \\\\ K & ={e}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the particular solution of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}=0$ that satisfies the condition $y(1)=e$ is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{2-1/x}\\,.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323695 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1346830d-69d5-5e73-b008-864e705c06e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2\\sqrt{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321295}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1346830d-69d5-5e73-b008-864e705c06e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2\\sqrt{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321295} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4536973-5a71-5025-9fa7-f6117f951929" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323487}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "79c0a17d-464c-5b60-a5f6-f47a7f26752e" :question/math-academy-id "q-355579" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{x}}$  that satisfies $y(1)={e}^{2}.$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find the particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & =2\\sqrt{x}+C\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the exponential function to both sides, we find the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|} & ={e}^{2\\sqrt{x}+C} \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{2\\sqrt{x}+C}\\, \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{2\\sqrt{x}}\\cdot {e}^{C} \\\\ y & =K{e}^{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is an arbitrary constant. \n\nWe now apply the condition $y(1)={e}^{2}$ to find $K:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2} & =K{e}^{2\\sqrt{1}} \\\\ {e}^{2} & =K{e}^{2} \\\\ K & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the particular solution of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{x}}$ that satisfies the condition $y(1)={e}^{2}$ is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332811 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e4267cc-bd1e-507b-8072-2b2a8c2eca09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{x}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330502} {:answer/id #uuid "8b417cb4-a6eb-5024-a1dc-ec0c00436d6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2x-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331187} {:answer/id #uuid "a75d6c04-3e75-57b3-9835-3be982051f16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331442} {:answer/id #uuid "bea71f13-c9fb-5f86-9a53-ebbcbb8c38fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331639} {:answer/id #uuid "ee34aca2-9235-5324-8e51-2b3c711ec180" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332039}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bea71f13-c9fb-5f86-9a53-ebbcbb8c38fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331639} :answer-field/id #uuid "d34e31ac-faf7-57f2-94a5-145454205303" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332572}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "35b7f010-fdff-57b3-be05-b9c6b80db56c" :question/math-academy-id "q-50220" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{y}^{2}=0$ that satisfies $y(1)=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find the particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-{y}^{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ -\\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{y} & =x+C \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{x+C}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{x+C},\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(1)=1$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =\\frac{1}{1+C} \\\\ 1+C & =1 \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the particular solution of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{y}^{2}=0$ that satisfies the condition $y(1)=1$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332825 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42390f84-76dd-513e-bfe6-30ef499d0b47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330541} {:answer/id #uuid "551e91b4-3be3-55eb-845f-b95b7bb7e037" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-y=\\sqrt{x+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330716} {:answer/id #uuid "625cb73d-fe58-5029-93a5-e925201b1fec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-y=\\sqrt{2x+7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330831} {:answer/id #uuid "a48b421b-d946-5b0e-a7fc-5f7cf21d83e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331427} {:answer/id #uuid "b2fdf6fa-d413-5479-9d2f-edfcccbd37ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331529}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2fdf6fa-d413-5479-9d2f-edfcccbd37ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331529} :answer-field/id #uuid "47c02f84-950c-5f01-8fca-af4deaa528ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332319}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "11569a87-05ce-5134-b8db-439ec10012e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-50566" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=x$  that satisfies  $y(-3)=1.$  Assume that $y>0.$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find the particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x \\\\ \\int y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int y\\text{d}y & =\\int x\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+{C}_{1} \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+2{C}_{1} \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+2{C}_{1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAssuming $y>0,$ the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+C},\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(-3)=1$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+C} \\\\ 1 & =\\sqrt{9+C} \\\\ C & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the particular solution of $y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=x$ that satisfies the condition $y(-3)=1$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-8}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351480 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30133227-afe7-5481-8b20-5e90c5357811" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348873} {:answer/id #uuid "8dc6e6b6-7795-585f-872f-9399fa1852a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349722} {:answer/id #uuid "9fbd50f1-5563-5199-adb8-f88d72b3446a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349870} {:answer/id #uuid "dfced686-1189-5123-b1b4-8db75ac9b85e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350409} {:answer/id #uuid "f802ca15-9874-59ee-8efd-bf70059a7581" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350620}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dfced686-1189-5123-b1b4-8db75ac9b85e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350409} :answer-field/id #uuid "643eee79-9b2d-5db9-9284-6c2e12ddc115" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350874}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4c64ef55-bd01-5eed-b990-f133790399ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-8308" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y$  that satisfies $y(0)=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find the particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & =x+C\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the exponential function to both sides, we find the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|} & ={e}^{x+C} \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{x+C}\\, \\\\ y & =\\pm {e}^{x+C} \\\\ y & =K{e}^{x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is an arbitrary constant. \n\nWe now apply the condition $y(0)=1$ to find $K:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =K{e}^{0} \\\\ K & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the particular solution of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y$ that satisfies the condition $y(0)=1$ is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{x}\\,.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351481 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fa50117-feef-5fb7-b4c7-8d5074635199" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y)=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348870} {:answer/id #uuid "452eaa99-839b-51b9-a716-4597a4aef31a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y)=2(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349076} {:answer/id #uuid "74fe7c6f-36b4-5a32-8184-47101e4f7568" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y)=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349521} {:answer/id #uuid "80940eb3-3340-5a50-8f91-c8c22404f7b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y)=2(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349628} {:answer/id #uuid "b1b526ac-6987-52e6-b725-e6fce574b42d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350018}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80940eb3-3340-5a50-8f91-c8c22404f7b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y)=2(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349628} :answer-field/id #uuid "aae6146f-444d-5de3-893c-8276bf7fa71f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351001}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "61f94262-435d-5c53-971b-8da63f6d82a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-8309" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y)}$ that satisfies $y(0)=0.$ Assume that $-\\frac{\\pi }{4}<y<\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y)} \\\\ (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y))\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x) \\\\ \\int (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y))\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y)\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y) & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+C \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y) & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+{C}_{1}\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1}=2C.$\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(0)=0$ to find ${C}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot 0) & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot 0)+{C}_{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(0) & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+{C}_{1} \\\\ 0 & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 1+{C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{1} & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we get the particular solution in implicit form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y) & =\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the above equation as\n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y)=2(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371852 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "158f36f0-5c9f-5df4-b307-3df00e1927ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{\\pi +2-4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371854} {:answer/id #uuid "394931cf-7701-52c5-939f-4d7925927a5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{4}{\\pi +4-4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371855} {:answer/id #uuid "8a89762a-446c-538c-a5f1-db6c984332b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{\\pi +1-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371856} {:answer/id #uuid "20d10a3f-7efd-565e-8b3b-25e4aed18fc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{\\pi -4+4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371857} {:answer/id #uuid "38fc6ca0-2882-53b5-a94f-8c1db20782e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{4}{\\pi -1-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371858}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "394931cf-7701-52c5-939f-4d7925927a5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{4}{\\pi +4-4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371855} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e6da82b-caa9-5a2a-8f75-9c0f26f5be8b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371853}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a14aaf63-21a2-55cb-ab96-06f1083b96bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-14309" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+1}$  that satisfies $y(1)=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find the particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -\\frac{1}{y} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{y} & =-(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+C) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+C}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+C}.\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(1)=1$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)+C} \\\\ 1 & =-\\frac{1}{\\frac{\\pi }{4}+C} \\\\ C & =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the particular solution of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+1}$ that satisfies the condition $y(1)=1$ is\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)-\\frac{\\pi }{4}-1}=\\frac{4}{\\pi +4-4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375167 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4da866c3-3d7f-5b0b-a810-ac33ce6a862f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{ln}^{2}⁡y=(1-x)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375169} {:answer/id #uuid "ff36e7bd-a5e4-5f39-b596-7ff7d49622b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{ln}^{2}⁡y=1-2{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375170} {:answer/id #uuid "b064fdd0-a36c-5cee-92e7-9e10247d699e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{ln}^{2}⁡y=1-{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375171} {:answer/id #uuid "7c31dc57-4c05-53a8-8a8e-94c43bdec15e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{ln}^{2}⁡y={x}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375172} {:answer/id #uuid "86e781a0-f04d-53d1-ab6b-bd9ecd3bb5ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{ln}^{2}⁡y=2{x}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375173}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b064fdd0-a36c-5cee-92e7-9e10247d699e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{ln}^{2}⁡y=1-{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375171} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8c617fb-7a13-5c0b-b392-e9e8047bec0c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375168}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0ff30466-1dca-57e2-9c20-c453c1fbb920" :question/math-academy-id "q-50219" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{xy}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}$ that satisfies $y(0)=e.$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find the particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{xy}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡y} \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =-\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}{y}\\text{d}y & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+{C}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the integral in the left-hand side of this equality, we use a substitution $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y.$ Then,  we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}y}=\\frac{1}{y}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nSo we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}{y}\\text{d}y & =\\int \\operatorname{ln}⁡y(\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =\\int u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}+{C}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{ln}^{2}⁡y+{C}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{ln}^{2}⁡y+{C}_{2} & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+{C}_{1} \\\\ {ln}^{2}⁡y & =-{x}^{2}+2({C}_{1}-{C}_{2}) \\\\ {ln}^{2}⁡y & =C-{x}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=2({C}_{1}-{C}_{2})$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(0)=e$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{ln}^{2}⁡e & =C-{0}^{2} \\\\ {1}^{2} & =C \\\\ C & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we get the particular solution in implicit form:\n\n$$\n{ln}^{2}⁡y=1-{x}^{2}.\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Particular Solutions of Differential Equations by Separating the Variables"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100894 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100732 :question/id #uuid "891e9004-961b-539a-ab93-0752347fd077" :question/math-academy-id "e-541" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\cos(3y)\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + \\sin(2x) = 0$ given that  $y\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) = 0.$ Assume that $-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6} < y < \\dfrac{\\pi}{6} .$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution, and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3y)\\,\\text{d}y & =-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3y) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+C \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3y) & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+{C}_{1}\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C_1=3C.$\n\nNow let's apply the condition $y\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) = 0$ to find $C_1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot 0) & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})+{C}_{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(0) & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+{C}_{1} \\\\ 0 & =0+{C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{1} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we get the particular solution in implicit form:\n\n$$\n\\sin(3y)   =  \\dfrac {3} {2} \\cos(2x)\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "de2e3a7c-d4f0-5b0f-8014-1519fb3a9473" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101297 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00ad7b38-011b-5bbc-ad6f-119764a28f5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{4x}-\\frac{1}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284794} {:answer/id #uuid "5cd60163-91c2-5f68-b12b-44f98e6c673c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{4x}+5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285640} {:answer/id #uuid "60978d4b-4f9a-549a-a78c-7b2ab10d8ff1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{4}+C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285674} {:answer/id #uuid "675c44fb-e5b5-5cc4-a665-61ee54152b6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{e}^{4x}+4{e}^{5}-1}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285732} {:answer/id #uuid "dd44c23d-4f4e-5432-b33e-dff52c07b119" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{4}+{e}^{5}+\\frac{1}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286799}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "675c44fb-e5b5-5cc4-a665-61ee54152b6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{e}^{4x}+4{e}^{5}-1}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285732} :answer-field/id #uuid "af959d06-569d-5cc3-8e3f-fadee181c763" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287510}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e844aafc-6198-5e85-8b96-36f86924e412" :question/math-academy-id "q-20071" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{4x-y}$ that satisfies $y(0)=5.$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{4x-y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{4x}\\cdot {e}^{-y} \\\\ {e}^{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{4x} \\\\ \\int {e}^{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int {e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {e}^{y} & =\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{4}+C\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the natural logarithm to both sides, we find the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{y}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{4}+C) \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{4}+C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(0)=5$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =5 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{e}^{4\\cdot 0}}{4}+C) & =5 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{4}+C) & =5 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}+C & ={e}^{5} \\\\ C & =\\frac{4{e}^{5}-1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the particular solution of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{4x-y}$ that satisfies the condition $y(0)=5$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{e}^{4x}+4{e}^{5}-1}{4}).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101539 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "289b6d64-a211-5da5-9a4e-e626db7e48e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{4{e}^{x}}{x+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345715} {:answer/id #uuid "539c1b15-7731-56bd-9c2a-ca623649b96c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{e}^{x+1}}{x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346061} {:answer/id #uuid "5e3ba5d5-d097-5032-a55f-f8306cff701c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2{e}^{x}}{x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346163} {:answer/id #uuid "9cfbb45a-ec8c-5ace-9f06-4292bd88e210" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2{e}^{-x}}{{x}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346719} {:answer/id #uuid "adf0d1d2-3ce8-5813-906f-ee26fca0599b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{4{e}^{-x}}{{x}^{2}+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346869}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e3ba5d5-d097-5032-a55f-f8306cff701c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2{e}^{x}}{x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346163} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c1ace9b-199b-55d1-97e0-bde3eb07a2d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347675}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "167e1aa1-119e-56a2-b6bf-0adbf44240f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-79372" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $(\\frac{1}{xy}+\\frac{1}{y})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=1$ that satisfies $y(1)=e.$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{1}{xy}+\\frac{1}{y})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}(\\frac{1+x}{x})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{x}{x+1} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{x}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int (1-\\frac{1}{x+1})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & =x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the natural logarithm to both sides, we find the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|} & ={e}^{x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C} \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C} \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|}\\cdot {e}^{C} \\\\ y & =\\frac{K{e}^{x}}{x+1},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is an arbitrary constant. \n\nWe now apply the initial condition $y(1)=e$ to find $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & =e \\\\ \\frac{K{e}^{1}}{1+1} & =e \\\\ K & =2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the particular solution of $(\\frac{1}{xy}+\\frac{1}{y})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=1$ that satisfies the condition $y(1)=e$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2{e}^{x}}{x+1}\\,.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235352 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d913ad1f-de16-5603-834b-341786551f9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2/({x}^{2}-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234884}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d913ad1f-de16-5603-834b-341786551f9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2/({x}^{2}-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234884} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3011aa0-ebf7-5b93-b9a2-848f373cb2d6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235190}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "93ff4d9b-f05d-54cb-bfec-91c45b5200dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-128192" :question/problem "Find the solution to the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x{y}^{2},\\,y(1)=2.\n$$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $y(x)=\\,\\,$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "From part 6, we found the general solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-2{({x}^{2}+K)}^{-1},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNow, we apply the initial condition $y(1)=2$ to find $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & =2 \\\\ -2{({1}^{2}+K)}^{-1} & =2 \\\\ 1+K & =-1 \\\\ K & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-2({x}^{2}-2)^{-1}=-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}-2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240790 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "158257b0-40d3-5bb7-bac5-6e36056355d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|-y=2{x}^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238234} {:answer/id #uuid "16004206-d689-5265-94c9-1376fc2a7419" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|={x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238241} {:answer/id #uuid "41e7778c-2d5c-54b6-9c23-07562072de44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|+y=2{x}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238628} {:answer/id #uuid "77b24ff7-d9d9-52ef-a8e8-e1a351aed83f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|-y={x}^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239088} {:answer/id #uuid "dd57c394-1382-5901-b867-e989f439cece" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|+y={x}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239988}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77b24ff7-d9d9-52ef-a8e8-e1a351aed83f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|-y={x}^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239088} :answer-field/id #uuid "30ba2846-d824-5e4c-aa9d-3131e60267bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240419}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cf490795-4587-5d21-a17e-b28b1511e0a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-104058" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $(\\frac{1}{xy}-\\frac{1}{x})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2$ that satisfies $y(1)=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{1}{xy}-\\frac{1}{x})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{x}(\\frac{1}{y}-1)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2 \\\\ (\\frac{1}{y}-1)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x \\\\ \\int (\\frac{1}{y}-1)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|-y & ={x}^{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now apply the initial condition $y(1)=1$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1|-1 & ={1}^{2}+C \\\\ 0-1 & =1+C \\\\ C & =-2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the particular solution of $(\\frac{1}{xy}-\\frac{1}{x})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2$ that satisfies the condition $y(1)=1$ in implicit form is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|-y={x}^{2}-2\\,.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240791 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c2258f7-1c89-5ddf-b3f5-be41b960a73f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{1+x-{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238450} {:answer/id #uuid "783d1f44-8f84-5b7c-88aa-9989c97b87f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{2+2x-{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239096} {:answer/id #uuid "96fd893a-6a0d-574c-96a1-9c7c9ecdd73f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{2x+{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239359} {:answer/id #uuid "c1af356d-96a3-5dbc-a694-254027046ada" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239753} {:answer/id #uuid "e73db678-16bb-58f9-861b-b9f4c455ff5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{x}{1+x+{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240090}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c2258f7-1c89-5ddf-b3f5-be41b960a73f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{1+x-{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238450} :answer-field/id #uuid "288ef88e-ee76-5cb6-ba28-c9169ad97783" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240401}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5f5fc67b-8b37-59a0-97e4-36b78ca0108f" :question/math-academy-id "q-104059" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+{y}^{2}$ that satisfies $y(-1)=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+{y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\text{d}y & =\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -\\frac{1}{y} & =-\\frac{1}{x}+x+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{y} & =\\frac{1-{x}^{2}-Cx}{x} \\\\ y & =\\frac{x}{1-{x}^{2}-Cx}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{x}{1-{x}^{2}-Cx},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant. \n\nWe now apply the initial condition $y(-1)=1$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(-1) & =1 \\\\ \\frac{-1}{1-(-1)^{2}-C(-1)} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{-1}{C} & =1 \\\\ C & =-1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the particular solution of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+{y}^{2}$ that satisfies the condition $y(-1)=1$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{x}{1+x-{x}^{2}}\\,.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240793 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "481424ef-2c74-5499-af3e-08e3dea9c7d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}-{x}^{4}+16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238682} {:answer/id #uuid "53af9c48-3c28-5879-bbf4-c5ba3f2cb694" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}-16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238773} {:answer/id #uuid "5acef49e-76b6-5522-ba50-3922470f8f96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238841} {:answer/id #uuid "b94a09be-43ba-5aaa-bb0c-be4b94983aec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239666} {:answer/id #uuid "e2fc50d5-241e-5e44-b681-3a645594909e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}-{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240052}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2fc50d5-241e-5e44-b681-3a645594909e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}-{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240052} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ffbe1bf-6c6b-54f5-a9b6-580dcd3d52fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240473}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "04d08db5-75c0-5a9e-a376-151cc06be633" :question/math-academy-id "q-104064" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3{y}^{2}{x}^{2}-4{y}^{2}{x}^{3}$ that satisfies $y(2)=\\frac{1}{8}.$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3{y}^{2}{x}^{2}-4{y}^{2}{x}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={y}^{2}(3{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (3{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y & =3\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-4\\int {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -\\frac{1}{y} & ={x}^{3}-{x}^{4}+C \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}-{x}^{3}-C}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now apply the initial condition $y(2)=\\frac{1}{8}$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(2) & =\\frac{1}{8} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{2}^{4}-{2}^{3}-C} & =\\frac{1}{8} \\\\ \\frac{1}{8-C} & =\\frac{1}{8} \\\\ C & =0\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the particular solution of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3{y}^{2}{x}^{2}-4{y}^{2}{x}^{3}$ that satisfies the condition $y(2)=\\frac{1}{8}$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}-{x}^{3}}\\,.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240794 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75be7c90-32ab-5c64-906d-1dd7954d2121" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+4{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239071} {:answer/id #uuid "82693f98-0710-5635-895b-2a14d84f4e9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4{x}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239189} {:answer/id #uuid "cd7da46f-6bf6-5179-ad99-a6c9aa84ab9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+8{x}^{2}-{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239852} {:answer/id #uuid "f3ba6483-6e8b-5e55-93bc-ce43b1b86246" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+8{x}^{2}-{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240204} {:answer/id #uuid "f3c87060-4f09-5b9a-ba36-024d4f3b3691" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+4{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240205}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75be7c90-32ab-5c64-906d-1dd7954d2121" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+4{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239071} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c9d1e6a-7b24-50e3-9ef5-4dad3d944987" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240555}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cdddaa9b-4174-550d-a7b6-1b8e1a3f2b29" :question/math-academy-id "q-104065" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-8x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ that satisfies $y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})={\\pi }^{2}.$ Assume that $0<x<\\pi .$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-8x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+8x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+8x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+8x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x+8\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x+4{x}^{2}+{C}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the integral on the right-hand side of this equality, we use a substitution\n\n$$\nu=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThen  we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+{C}_{2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+{C}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we used that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+4{x}^{2}+C.\n$$\n\nwhere $C={C}_{1}+{C}_{2}$ is an arbitrary constant. \n\nWe now apply the initial condition $y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})={\\pi }^{2}$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & ={\\pi }^{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2})+4{(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}+C & ={\\pi }^{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡1+{\\pi }^{2}+C & ={\\pi }^{2} \\\\ C & =0\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the particular solution of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-8x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ that satisfies the condition $y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})={\\pi }^{2}$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+4{x}^{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272362 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a4171c5-a734-541a-8466-69b56ce0d873" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{e}^{x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269359} {:answer/id #uuid "38ecd45c-ba43-5032-b302-2a4db3f68db3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x+{e}^{x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269785} {:answer/id #uuid "b0aa3d76-7de4-57d1-9a49-fedf8212ceab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+{e}^{x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270846} {:answer/id #uuid "b2780b36-6d65-5d14-9b03-a066bbecba37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-2x-{e}^{x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270864} {:answer/id #uuid "bcf951d6-95fc-5d3d-8bf4-6811a7297a18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-{e}^{x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270934}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bcf951d6-95fc-5d3d-8bf4-6811a7297a18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-{e}^{x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270934} :answer-field/id #uuid "c146e3ac-1df2-5b81-bf01-9382213bcac9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271861}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4a8c132a-a85d-5f19-bf08-0d6e90b34efd" :question/math-academy-id "q-14267" :question/problem "Find the specific solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x+y}$ given that  $\\,y(0)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{x+y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{x}{e}^{y} \\\\ {e}^{-y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{x} \\\\ \\int {e}^{-y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {e}^{-y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -{e}^{-y} & ={e}^{x}+{C}_{1} \\\\ {e}^{-y} & =-{e}^{x}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the natural logarithm to both sides, we find the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-y}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-{e}^{x}+C) \\\\ -y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(C-{e}^{x}) \\\\ y & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(C-{e}^{x}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(0)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡(C-{e}^{0}) & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(C-1) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ C-1 & =2 \\\\ C & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the particular solution of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x+y}$ that satisfies the condition $y(0)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$ is\n\n$$\ny=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-{e}^{x}).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320759 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4fe73586-88cc-5eb9-b551-265a21f8d21c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}(2{x}^{3}-x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319625}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4fe73586-88cc-5eb9-b551-265a21f8d21c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}(2{x}^{3}-x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319625} :answer-field/id #uuid "138ff64c-aba6-5818-815e-c8ab6507d894" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320202}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1e6d2e79-6130-55ee-82e5-9a200a11282e" :question/math-academy-id "q-350957" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{e}^{-y}=6{x}^{2}{e}^{-y}$ that satisfies $y(1)=0.$\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{e}^{-y} & =6{x}^{2}{e}^{-y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =6{x}^{2}{e}^{-y}-{e}^{-y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =(6{x}^{2}-1){e}^{-y} \\\\ {e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =(6{x}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int (6{x}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {e}^{y} & =2{x}^{3}-x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the natural logarithm to both sides, we find the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{y}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{3}-x+C) \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{3}-x+C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now apply the initial condition $y(1)=0$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\cdot {1}^{3}-1+C) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+C) & =0 \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{3}-x).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323687 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f64a635e-2029-5d66-8938-817f402fbc99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{x}+4{x}^{2}+7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323077}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f64a635e-2029-5d66-8938-817f402fbc99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{x}+4{x}^{2}+7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323077} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d3128ea-a07c-5f2f-ad3f-00a6446136d1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323290}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6e98fc5d-ad7f-574e-b282-efe5e38c1e1b" :question/math-academy-id "q-355546" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation ${x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-8{x}^{3}=5$ that satisfies $y(1)=6.$\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-8{x}^{3} & =5 \\\\ {x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =5+8{x}^{3} \\\\ \\text{d}y & =(5{x}^{-2}+8x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\text{d}y & =\\int (5{x}^{-2}+8x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =-5{x}^{-1}+4{x}^{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now apply the initial condition $y(1)=6$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & =6 \\\\ -5(1)^{-1}+4(1)^{2}+C & =6 \\\\ C-1 & =6 \\\\ C & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =-5{x}^{-1}+4{x}^{2}+7 \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{x}+4{x}^{2}+7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323688 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13eaf0de-4e0e-5ea8-907d-4d9968125b18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{x}+2{x}^{2}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321308}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13eaf0de-4e0e-5ea8-907d-4d9968125b18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{x}+2{x}^{2}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321308} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc4ee59c-22f5-5a40-b03d-278d405cc912" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323609}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "75023361-eb11-537e-8a6b-dbd0dee1ad40" :question/math-academy-id "q-355547" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\sqrt{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-4x\\sqrt{x}=3$ that satisfies $y(1)=11.$\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-4x\\sqrt{x} & =3 \\\\ \\sqrt{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3+4x\\sqrt{x} \\\\ \\text{d}y & =(3{x}^{-1/2}+4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\text{d}y & =\\int (3{x}^{-1/2}+4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =6{x}^{1/2}+2{x}^{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now apply the initial condition $y(1)=11$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & =11 \\\\ 6(1)^{1/2}+2(1)^{2}+C & =11 \\\\ C+8 & =11 \\\\ C & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =6{x}^{1/2}+2{x}^{2}+3 \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{x}+2{x}^{2}+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323689 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2055e3dd-2e6d-5b82-b38d-ca3921647471" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2{e}^{2{x}^{2}}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321390}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2055e3dd-2e6d-5b82-b38d-ca3921647471" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2{e}^{2{x}^{2}}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321390} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f37f1d2-7d30-5928-8c23-707d4ffb2cc7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323197}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "af7393d2-efb0-5146-b448-33e99ed4412d" :question/math-academy-id "q-355559" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-4x{y}^{2}=4x$ that satisfies $y(0)=1.$ Assume that $y>0.$\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-4x{y}^{2} & =4x \\\\ 2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =4x{y}^{2}+4x \\\\ 2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =4x({y}^{2}+1) \\\\ \\frac{2y}{{y}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}y & =4x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{2y}{{y}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int 4x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1) & =2{x}^{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExponentiating both sides, we find the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1)} & ={e}^{2{x}^{2}+C} \\\\ {y}^{2}+1 & =C{e}^{2{x}^{2}} \\\\ y & =\\sqrt{C{e}^{2{x}^{2}}-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we used that $y>0.$\n\nWe now apply the initial condition $y(0)=1$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =1 \\\\ \\sqrt{C{e}^{2(0)^{2}}-1} & =1 \\\\ \\sqrt{C-1} & =1 \\\\ C-1 & =1 \\\\ C & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)=\\sqrt{2{e}^{2{x}^{2}}-1}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323691 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11d2d065-70d1-5174-a4e1-728f26ee993f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x-1){e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321286}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11d2d065-70d1-5174-a4e1-728f26ee993f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x-1){e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321286} :answer-field/id #uuid "a70e7a74-f88a-5b4f-8fac-b4f7d571a35d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323490}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aabfb888-4d5b-5ee3-a31d-caf8a930a357" :question/math-academy-id "q-355564" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $(-\\frac{1}{xy}+\\frac{2}{y})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4$ that satisfies $y(1)={e}^{2}.$\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-\\frac{1}{xy}+\\frac{2}{y})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =4 \\\\ \\frac{2x-1}{xy}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =4 \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{4x}{2x-1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2+\\frac{2}{2x-1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =(2+\\frac{2}{2x-1})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int (2+\\frac{2}{2x-1})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & =2x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the exponential function to both sides, we find the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|} & ={e}^{2x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|+C} \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{2x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|+C} \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{2x}\\cdot {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|}\\cdot {e}^{C} \\\\ y & =K(2x-1){e}^{2x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is an arbitrary constant. \n\nWe now apply the initial condition $y(1)={e}^{2}$ to find $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & ={e}^{2} \\\\ K(2(1)-1){e}^{2(1)} & ={e}^{2} \\\\ K{e}^{2} & ={e}^{2} \\\\ K & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)=(2x-1){e}^{2x}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323692 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90c5bd0e-992a-5060-a1b1-7adb29cef3ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-{e}^{-2x}+2{e}^{2}+1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322298}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90c5bd0e-992a-5060-a1b1-7adb29cef3ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-{e}^{-2x}+2{e}^{2}+1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322298} :answer-field/id #uuid "15f6b0da-0d33-5c51-ac9b-a4198b00fb73" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323209}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "805542c0-bca8-5f60-bf27-701c39eb6996" :question/math-academy-id "q-355576" :question/problem "Find the specific solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{-2x-y}$ given that  $\\,y(0)=2.$\n\n$y(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-1}} $)$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{-2x-y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{-2x}{e}^{-y} \\\\ {e}^{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{-2x} \\\\ \\int {e}^{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{-2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int {e}^{-2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {e}^{y} & =-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-2x}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the natural logarithm to both sides, we find the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{y}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-2x}+C) \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-2x}+C).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(0)=2$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =2 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{0}+C) & =2 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(C-\\frac{1}{2}) & =2 \\\\ C-\\frac{1}{2} & ={e}^{2} \\\\ C & =\\frac{2{e}^{2}+1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the particular solution of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{-2x-y}$ that satisfies the condition $y(0)=2$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{-{e}^{-2x}+2{e}^{2}+1}{2}).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323693 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7cecbd4b-7276-5711-93be-756561f37cee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}(\\frac{5}{4}-{e}^{-x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322129}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7cecbd4b-7276-5711-93be-756561f37cee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}(\\frac{5}{4}-{e}^{-x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322129} :answer-field/id #uuid "19779a17-730f-59fa-bfa1-c2bc8e54d880" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323218}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a6ea94de-6671-5cd2-9382-54cb9545def9" :question/math-academy-id "q-355577" :question/problem "Find the specific solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{-x-y}$ given that $\\,y(0)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4.$\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{-x-y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{-x}{e}^{-y} \\\\ {e}^{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{-x} \\\\ \\int {e}^{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {e}^{y} & =-{e}^{-x}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the natural logarithm to both sides, we find the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{y}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-{e}^{-x}+C) \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-{e}^{-x}+C).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(0)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(-{e}^{0}+C) & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(C-1) & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4 \\\\ C-1 & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ C & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the particular solution of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{-x-y}$ that satisfies the condition $y(0)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{4}-{e}^{-x}).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323696 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d94f85d-4906-5228-9ed0-d96a48b11c86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}(2-{e}^{-x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321501}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d94f85d-4906-5228-9ed0-d96a48b11c86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}(2-{e}^{-x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321501} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e8e425b-6779-5e93-9532-18cad7118f4f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323392}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b704df68-ffc0-57d3-acee-bb78600fdc0b" :question/math-academy-id "q-355582" :question/problem "Find the specific solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{-x-y}$ given that  $\\,y(0)=0.$\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{-x-y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{-x}{e}^{-y} \\\\ {e}^{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{-x} \\\\ \\int {e}^{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {e}^{y} & =-{e}^{-x}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the natural logarithm to both sides, we find the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{y}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-{e}^{-x}+C) \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-{e}^{-x}+C).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(0)=0$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(-{e}^{0}+C) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(C-1) & =0 \\\\ C-1 & =1 \\\\ C & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the particular solution of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{-x-y}$ that satisfies the condition $y(0)=0$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-{e}^{-x}).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326809 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "502004df-a0be-5cea-878e-09803c5e34d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{e}^{x}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324628} {:answer/id #uuid "5babf9ef-2007-5ac3-b1a7-d3a7000142a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-{e}^{x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324728} {:answer/id #uuid "9f32f367-c166-57dd-a1ca-a5e258aafc79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325321} {:answer/id #uuid "cb26728e-8a5c-5d88-940f-50ca2ae95d4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{e}^{x}+1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325734} {:answer/id #uuid "f04334c1-fe98-564e-8bda-076b98d63f11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4{e}^{x}-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326091}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "502004df-a0be-5cea-878e-09803c5e34d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{e}^{x}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324628} :answer-field/id #uuid "2fa3b410-49d2-532c-b067-882d1961f404" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326303}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "45459917-ee60-5543-8543-ded7c1f55344" :question/math-academy-id "q-4016" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x-2y}$ that satisfies $y(0)=0.$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{x-2y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{-2y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2y}} \\\\ {e}^{2y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{x} \\\\ \\int {e}^{2y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {e}^{2y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2y} & ={e}^{x}+{C}_{1} \\\\ {e}^{2y} & =2{e}^{x}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=2{C}_{1}$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nApplying the natural logarithm to both sides, we find the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2y}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{e}^{x}+C) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{e}^{x}+C).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(0)=0$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{e}^{0}+C) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+C) & =0 \\\\ 2+C & =1 \\\\ C & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the particular solution of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x-2y}$ that satisfies the condition $y(0)=0$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{e}^{x}-1).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348217 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c251d4a-f0c0-5a48-8e3e-7592f3c7a8d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x+{e}^{-3x}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345744} {:answer/id #uuid "5e801574-e220-5db2-a084-a19a5433b090" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{2}-3{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346166} {:answer/id #uuid "781de7e7-abc0-5010-a18b-6acb28d569ae" :answer/type {:db/ident